{
  "name": "camels",
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "lay_j0crme",
      "type": "symbols",
      "name": "camels symbols",
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_bfn9se", "address": "$0033", "text": "camelMode - the player state machine, complete. ControlCamel reads the joystick only in mode 0; every other mode runs an animation in $94F6 and ignores the stick.\n\n  0  on the ground. Left/right walk, up starts a jump, down starts a duck, fire shoots.\n  1  airborne. jumpVelocity ($39) counts $0F down through $00 to $F0 and is added to objYPos as a SIGNED value, so sixteen steps up and sixteen down. At $F0 the camel lands: voice 3 gets a short triangle thud, mode becomes 5 and jumpVelocity is reset to 5.\n  5  landing squash. jumpVelocity counts 4,3,2,1 and the sprite shape comes from the four bytes at $9565 - CE CF CF CE - so the camel compresses and springs back. At zero the shape returns to $C0 and the mode to 0.\n  2  ducking down. INC the sprite shape until it reaches $D2, then mode 3.\n  3  held down. Stays here until the player releases down, or until objXPos reaches $11, the left wall.\n  4  standing up. DEC the sprite shape back to $CD, then shape $C0, mode 0, and clear objMoveType.\n\nSo the whole animation vocabulary is a contiguous run of sprite blocks: $C0 standing, $C0-$CC the walk cycle stepped by $8C2F, $CD jumping, $CE-$D2 the duck, and $CE/$CF again for the landing squash.\n\nFiring is allowed in modes 0, 1 and 5 and refused in 2, 3 and 4 - you cannot shoot while crouched." },
        { "id": "cmt_9cbf73", "address": "$0050", "text": "Difficulty loop counter. Incremented at $9DA4 when all 42 zones are cleared and clamped to 5 at $9DAD, so the game gets harder five times and then stops getting harder. Also read at $9701 to shorten a timer: $43 = 12 - 2*loopNumber." },
        { "id": "cmt_952lix", "address": "$0056", "text": "Zero page selector, seen compared against 1 and 2 (sub_539B, msg suffix patching) and against 2 alone (sub_5359, loc_561E). Chooses between player1_state/player2_state, and between the ONE/TWO suffix in msg_stand_by_player, msg_beasts_extinct and msg_enter_your_name. Zeroed once at cold start ($8A3C); where it is set to 1 or 2 has not been traced yet." },
        { "id": "cmt_pzi5v8", "address": "$0056", "placement": "after", "text": "Correction/addition: on the title-options screen (around $525E-$528D) this same location is toggled 0/1 with INC + AND #$01 to highlight a 1 PLAYER / 2 PLAYER choice, and is read as a plain zero/nonzero flag at $525E. So it holds 0/1 here but 1/2 once a game is running (sub_5359 etc compare it to 1 and 2). Either the menu writes 1/2 before starting the round, or there are two related-but-distinct uses of the same byte across game phases -- not confirmed which." },
        { "id": "cmt_zeztxf", "address": "$0058", "text": "Current zone, 0-41. Compared with #$2A at $9DA0, which is what fixes the game at 42 zones." },
        { "id": "cmt_9v05la", "address": "$0059", "text": "Boolean, toggled by F1 (matrix code 4) on the options screen (loc_51EB/loc_5271, EOR #$FF at $527B). When set, zone selection at loc_937B draws RandomByteFromBASICROM AND #$1F (0-31) into $58, the zone-index variable the NEXINI stepping loop reads - confirms the credits text at $5B73 (\"next wave randomly drawn from any of the first 32\") exactly: zones 33-42 (index 32-41) can never be chosen by random mode, only by playing through sequentially or by the dead $C000 cheat basalt found." },
        { "id": "cmt_c40zum", "address": "$0340", "text": "Screen row address tables, low bytes at $0340 and high bytes at $0360, used by ScreenAddrFromRow. They live in the tape buffer and are BUILT AT RUNTIME by sub_87FE, so the loaded image has nothing useful here - reading $0340 through this project shows whatever the decruncher left behind, not the table.\n\n27 entries, $0400 stepping by 40. Entries 25 and 26 point past the screen at $07E8 and $0810." },
        { "id": "cmt_8gijfu", "address": "$0400", "text": "Screen row 0 is the status line. InitStatusLine ($544C) fills rows 0-1 from txtStatusLineTemplate ($90F8), 61 chars:\n  \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \" then \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  H\"\nSo the score is the 7 digits at $040E-$0414 (scoreField+1..+7), which is exactly what CheckHighScore ($5544) compares. Colour RAM for the screen is screen+$D400 (see PrintCharAt $883A)." },
        { "id": "cmt_901i53", "address": "$0400", "text": "THE WHOLE SCREEN LAYOUT, put together from five places that each know one row of it. Screen $0400, colour $D800, 40x25.\n\n  row 0     $0400  status line: \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \"\n                   score = seven ASCII digits at $040E-$0414\n                   distance = two digits at $0421-$0422\n  row 1     $0428  \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  \" then the 18-cell fuel bar at $043D-$044E\n  rows 2-11        sky. The twelve stars live here - starInitRow at $8A1A is 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 02 0B 06 07 08, all inside this band\n  rows 9-10 $0590  messageLine, where BEASTS REMAINING and the zone banner are printed\n  rows 14-19 $0630 $0658 $0680 $06A8 $06D0 $06F8  THE SCROLLING TERRAIN, six rows, shifted one column left by the loop at $8C53 with their colour rows $DA30-$DAF8 alongside\n  rows 20-24 $0720  ground, filled with character $28 in colour 6 by the loop at $8AC6\n\nThe six terrain rows and the five ground rows are why $D016 has CSEL cleared at $8871 - 38 columns opens the side border so a column can be shifted in without showing the seam. The fine scroll is $1F counting 7 down to 0 and the coarse shift happens when it wraps, at $8C4D.\n\nSprites are drawn over all of it and the camel walks on the boundary at row 20.\n\n(Added beside basalt earlier note rather than replacing it.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_axs6xp", "address": "$0421", "text": "The two digits of \"NEXT ZONE nn KM\" on the status line - offsets 33 and 34 of txtStatusLineTemplate. $9D2C decrements them as you travel ($9D35 DEC $0422, borrowing into $0421); $937B sets and carries them the other way. When they reach zero the zone is complete." },
        { "id": "cmt_gjgbsm", "address": "$043C", "text": "THE NEUTRONIUM STATUS BAR, on screen row 1 from column 21. Always addressed as $043C,X or $043C,Y with a 1-based index, so the first cell is $043D.\n\nWhy that address: InitStatusLine ($544C) copies $3D = 61 bytes from txtStatusLineTemplate ($90F8) into $0400, which is row 0 (40 chars) plus the first 21 of the second panel line at $9120 - so it fills $0400-$043C exactly and the bar starts at the next byte.\n\nEight sites move it and they are the self-modifying ones beryl found: $9C12 and $9EB8 among them have their OPCODE poked by $C023/$C046, so a static read of INC/DEC there tells you the addressing mode and not the direction. $9C0C grows it, $9EB6 shrinks it." },
        { "id": "cmt_hminb6", "address": "$043C", "text": "THE NEUTRONIUM BAR - the game main resource, and it is stored NOWHERE ELSE. It lives in screen memory: $043D to $044E, eighteen cells, row 1 columns 21-38, right under the words NEUTRONIUM STATUS. $043C is the base the code indexes 1-based.\n\nThat is why $9FAC fills it by writing #$48 to $043C,X for X = 1..$12, and why $9120 carries exactly eighteen H characters and $915E exactly eighteen colour bytes. Three independent places agree on eighteen.\n\nA cell holds a glyph between $48 and $50 - nine states of the same bar segment in the custom charset - and $20 when empty. DrainNeutronium at $9C0C advances one cell per call and empties it when it passes $50; $4A neutroniumLeft is the index of the cell currently being eaten, so it doubles as how much is left.\n\nStoring a resource in the display rather than in a variable is not a shortcut here, it is the whole design: the bar IS the number, and every routine that changes it does so by reading and writing screen RAM." },
        { "id": "cmt_satr2i", "address": "$0630", "text": "First of the six scrolling terrain rows: $0630 $0658 $0680 $06A8 $06D0 $06F8, rows 14-19, with colour rows $DA30 $DA58 $DA80 $DAA8 $DAD0 $DAF8. The loop at $8C53 copies each one byte left across all 39 columns, both character and colour, once every eight frames when the fine scroll at $1F wraps. New columns are pushed in at the right by EmitTerrainColumn from newColumnChars ($1FF0)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_deyq88",
      "type": "rom",
      "rom": "characters",
      "name": "characters rom"
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_fg504y",
      "type": "rom",
      "rom": "kernal",
      "name": "kernal rom"
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_ghrx9l",
      "type": "rom",
      "rom": "basic",
      "name": "basic rom"
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_5xtrg9",
      "type": "prg",
      "path": "revenge.d64:revenge fixed",
      "name": "packed"
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_0qqeu3",
      "type": "prg",
      "path": "runtime.prg",
      "name": "runtime",
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_69fdfc", "address": "$0801", "text": "probe: is this a hires/charset bitmap? checking visually before committing to a real claim" },
        { "id": "cmt_7mpdq3", "address": "$0801", "text": "Sprite blocks $20-$3F, the ones the game swaps. CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 copies a 2K bank here from $1000, $1800, $4000 or $4800 according to the index the wave descriptor supplies in $02. The bytes sitting here in the runtime image are whichever bank was live when the snapshot was taken." },
        { "id": "cmt_afpodt", "address": "$0801", "text": "32 x 63-byte hardware sprites (24x21). Tried view=char:8 (hires) first -- wrong shape, garbled. view=sprite renders cleanly. Sits in the normal BASIC program area; the game does not return to BASIC after its SYS stub, so this space is reused for sprite data. Biggest undecoded span in the image (18KB from $0801); only the first 2016 bytes (32 frames) are checked. Have not matched a sprite pointer table or identified which frame is which creature." },
        { "id": "cmt_dmqoe5", "address": "$0801", "text": "probe: sprite sheet?" },
        { "id": "cmt_jdwkpa", "address": "$0801", "text": "One large sprite sheet, ~288 standard 64-byte hi-res C64 sprites (24x21) back to back, from $0801 to $4FFF -- nothing in the code reached so far references it (no root existed before this claim; find_instructions on the VIC sprite-pointer range $07F8-$07FF and on STA $D000-$D010 turns up nothing yet, and it explicitly reports several indirect/computed-address writes it cannot resolve, so the code that actually points the VIC at a sprite number almost certainly lives in the still-undecoded $5E00-$87FD span). Visually (rendered in 16-wide grid chunks): recognisable camels in several walk/leg positions, small flying/aircraft-like enemies, falling parachute figures, devil/pitchfork and skeleton figures, a robot, the digits 0-9 and some letters, and a band roughly a third of the way through that renders as visual noise in this hi-res view -- possibly debris/explosion-particle sprites that are just genuinely busy-looking, possibly something not meant to be read as a plain hi-res sprite (multicolour mode, or a different frame boundary); not resolved. Could not tie any specific sprite index to a name (camel/enemy/digit-N) because the selecting code has not been found yet -- recorded only the visual grouping, not an index mapping." },
        { "id": "cmt_wqamr2", "address": "$0801", "text": "HOW THE SPRITE MEMORY IS SPLIT, read off all 42 zone records rather than guessed.\n\nEvery zone draws its creatures from two places:\n\n  blocks $20-$3F   $0800-$0FFF   THE SWAPPED BANK. Contents depend on which 2K CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 last copied here, chosen per zone: bank $1000 for zones 0-25, $1800 for 26-29, $4000 for 30-35, $4800 for 36-41. Three swaps in 42 zones.\n  blocks $90-$FF   $2400-$3FFF   RESIDENT. Always there, because the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF and a pointer can reach them without a copy.\n\nThe resident set is the shared vocabulary — the player camel at $C0 and $CD-$D2, the explosion at $EC-$EE which every single zone uses, the shot at $DA. The swapped bank is what makes zone 30 look different from zone 3.\n\nBlocks $40-$8F ($1000-$23FF) are never used as sprite pointers by any zone, which is consistent: $1000-$1FFF is bank source and variables, and $2000-$23FF is the font." },
        { "id": "cmt_xkaycx", "address": "$0801", "text": "placeholder - will replace comment next" },
        { "id": "cmt_ij1tav", "address": "$1000", "text": "2K sprite bank, 32 blocks of 64 bytes. Rendered: the camel, in walk-cycle frames. One of the sources CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 copies to $0800-$0FFF." },
        { "id": "cmt_m4i7ly", "address": "$1001", "text": "Byte-identical to $0801-$1000 (verified with two read_bytes calls, exact match, 2047 bytes checked). Sprite pointer 32 ($0800/64) and pointer 64 ($1000/64) fetch the same picture. Have not found any direct-addressed write into the second half of MultiplexSpritesThisFrames 16-slot field arrays (slots 9-16), which is what would actually make use of a second copy -- so this looks like either an unused second multiplexer bank or a plain editing duplicate." },
        { "id": "cmt_6g20xa", "address": "$1800", "text": "Sprite data. Rendered: creatures and ships. Note this bank runs out at $1D00 where the object variables begin, so only about 20 of its 32 blocks hold pictures — a copy of the full 2K from $1800 would drag variables along behind them, into blocks $3A-$3F, which nothing appears to display." },
        { "id": "cmt_9oecru", "address": "$1D00", "text": "Not graphics: 768 bytes of game state, 336 instructions reference it. Type templates, live object slots and the sprite multiplexer arrays. See the comments at $1D00, $1E40 and $1F40. It sits here because the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF and these are sprite blocks $74-$7F, which the game simply never displays." },
        { "id": "cmt_p1080s", "address": "$1D00", "text": "OBJECT SYSTEM. Two different things live in $1D00-$1FFF and they are indexed differently.\n\nTYPE-INDEXED (Y = 0..7, one entry per creature type in this wave):\n  $1D00 typeSpawnTimer  counts down; at zero sub_9744 spawns one of that type\n  $1DA0-$1E37          the WAVE DESCRIPTOR, 152 bytes = 19 tables of 8, copied\n                       wholesale from the level stream by loc_9699 (CPY #$98)\n\nSLOT-INDEXED (X = object slot, 16 wide):\n  $1D10 objType   which template this slot was made from (sub_9772 $97BF: TYA STA $1D10,X)\n  $1D20..$1D90, $1E40, $1E50, $1E70, $1E80, $1E90, $1FB0\n\nsub_9772 is the spawn: it copies one column of the template block into the matching slot arrays. That mapping is what named these:\n\n  tmplFieldFB0  $1DA8 -> objFieldFB0 $1FB0\n  tmplShape     $1DB0 -> objShape    $1D30  and sprShape $1F70\n  tmplField40   $1DB8 -> objField40  $1D40\n  tmplField50   $1DC0 -> objField50  $1D50, and AND #$3F -> objAnimBase $1D20\n  tmplSpeedX    $1DE0 -> objSpeedX   $1D60  and objBaseSpeedX $1E70\n  tmplSpeedY    $1DE8 -> objSpeedY   $1D70  and objBaseSpeedY $1E80\n  tmplField80   $1DF0 -> objField80  $1D80\n  tmplSpawnLimit$1DF8 -> objSpawnLimit $1D90\n  tmplColour    $1E18 -> sprColour   $1F60\n  tmplFieldE90  $1E20 -> objFieldE90 $1E90\n\n$1DF8 bit 7 means the type is capped: $9764 masks the low nibble and compares it with $4D, refusing the spawn once that many exist.\n\nloc_96B0 seeds $1D00-$1D07 from $1DA0-$1DA7 and then zeroes $1D08-$1D9F, which is the whole slot space, at wave start.\n\nThe field names ending in a bare address are honest: the copy proves they are one field, the meaning is not settled." },
        { "id": "cmt_u0bi81", "address": "$1D10", "text": "One byte per object slot (X, up to at least 16 slots -- sub_8B20 zeroes 16 of them at init). Holds the type/kind index of whatever occupies slot X, written by spawn_object_from_template ($9772) as TYA;STA dat_1D10,X right after copying that type's template fields. Read back all over the object-AI code ($9744,$97CB.. through $9C6B) as LDA/LDY dat_1D10,X to fetch Y = type, then Y indexes a further per-type table (e.g. dat_1DF8,Y) -- a two-level indirection: object slot -> type -> per-type property. This is the central \"one thing refers to another\" relation in the game logic." },
        { "id": "cmt_9pxw8p", "address": "$1DA0", "text": "THE CREATURE TEMPLATE BLOCK: nineteen tables of eight, 152 bytes, copied here from the zone record at $6700 + 200*zoneNumber by loc_9699. Eight creature types, nineteen attributes each. Every one of the nineteen is now accounted for except none — all read somewhere:\n\n  $1DA0 tmplSpawnInterval      how often one appears (-> typeSpawnTimer)\n  $1DA8 tmplMoveType           0 static, 1 constant-speed, 2 ballistic; bit 7 spare\n  $1DB0 tmplAnimFirst          first sprite block\n  $1DB8 tmplAnimLast           last sprite block\n  $1DC0 tmplAnimMode           bit6 ping-pong, low 6 the frame delay\n  $1DC8 tmplTypeWhenShot       become this when hit by the shot ($9B00)\n  $1DD0 tmplTypeWhenExploded   become this when it explodes ($9B63)\n  $1DD8 tmplNextType           become this when its lifetime runs out; $FF = die ($9A4F)\n  $1DE0 tmplSpeedX             see the movement handlers - meaning depends on tmplMoveType\n  $1DE8 tmplSpeedY             ditto\n  $1DF0 tmplField80            step size for handler 1, bounce countdown for handler 2\n  $1DF8 tmplSpawnLimit         bit 7 = capped; low nibble = how many may exist at once\n  $1E00 tmplSpawnX             starting X; 0 with mode $10 means random\n  $1E08 tmplSpawnY             starting Y; 0 with mode $10 means random\n  $1E10 tmplSpawnMode          0 fixed, $10 random-if-zero, anything else relative ($985E)\n  $1E18 tmplColour             sprite colour; bit 7 makes it cycle every frame\n  $1E20 tmplLifetime           ticks before tmplNextType takes over\n  $1E28 tmplScoreValue         points for shooting it\n  $1E30 tmplExplodeLength      explosion duration; 0 means it does not explode\n\nFOUR SEPARATE ROUTES from one creature to another — shot, exploded, aged out, and bounced out ($9A20) — all landing in the same sub_9772. That is the whole design: a wave is a directed graph of eight creature types, and the zone record is its adjacency list.\n\nVerified against real data. Wave 0 gives moveType all 1 or 2, animFirst/animLast all in $90-$EE which is sprite data inside residentSprites, nextType all 0-6 or $FF, colour all 0-15 with the odd bit 7. Nineteen columns of plausible values is not something a wrong stride produces." },
        { "id": "cmt_eg76cz", "address": "$1E40", "text": "objXPos / objYPos, and SLOT 0 IS THE PLAYER.\n\nloc_5049 reads objXPos,X, compares it with objXPos (element 0) and then sets bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX,X when the object is at or beyond the player and clears it when it is not, mirroring the result into objSpeedX,X. loc_506A does the same for Y through objBaseSpeedY / objSpeedY.\n\nThat is homing: bit 7 of the speed byte is its sign, and the creature is turned to face slot 0. It only makes sense if slot 0 is the thing being chased.\n\nCorroboration: ShowBeastsRemaining ($50DE) plants #$C0 into objXPos before drawing the status line, and $95B9 / loc_962E move objXPos+1 and objYPos+1 with INC and DEC, i.e. slot 1 under some other control.\n\nWhat I have NOT established: whether slot 0 is the player ship or the camel being defended. The game is about defending camels, so either reading is live." },
        { "id": "cmt_ey476l", "address": "$1F00", "text": "Starfield state, four 13-byte arrays indexed 1..12 (entry 0 unused, because the seed loop runs X = $0C down to 1 and stops before zero):\n  $1F00 column 0-39\n  $1F10 row\n  $1F20 glyph, counts $1F down to $1B\n  $1F30 delay, counts down to zero and then the star is updated\nWritten by $8AEA, stepped by UpdateStarfield at $8FB0, drawn by UpdateStar at $8FD2. The per-star delay is what gives the field parallax." },
        { "id": "cmt_2dyzit", "address": "$1F40", "text": "CORRECTION to the comment above, from amber, and I accept it: the multiplexer EXISTS AND NEVER RUNS.\n\nMy reading of the mechanism at $925F is right — two banks of state, frame parity in $30, sub_929D for even frames and sub_92CF for odd — but it never fires, because nothing ever allocates a slot in 8-15.\n\nThe evidence is the zone data. sub_92F6 is the only allocator and every caller passes Y in 0..7; FindFreeObjectSlot searches 2 to $42-1, and $42 read out of all 42 zone records is 8 in forty-one of them and 6 in zone 6. $1F89-$1F8F are never written after the initial clear. So the test at $9283 always finds sprInUse zero, always takes the sub_929D path, and the shadow arrays at $1F48/$1F58/$1F68/$1F78 are written by nothing.\n\nSo this is machinery for sixteen sprites that the game as shipped drives with eight — which is a fact about the program, not about the analysis, and exactly the sort of thing only reading the data settles. Keep the field names: they are correct about what the code would do.\n\n(One consequence for anyone reading further: the frame-1 enable mask sprEnableMaskFrame1 at $1F99 stays whatever CheckOptionsKey and sub_92F6 left in it, and it is ORed into $D015 on odd frames regardless.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_o9ew3u", "address": "$1F40", "text": "SPRITE MULTIPLEXER STATE. Sixteen virtual sprites on eight hardware ones, alternated frame by frame.\n\nloc_925F toggles $30 between 0 and 1 every frame and then, for hardware sprite X = 8..1, renders EITHER virtual sprite X-1 (arrays $1F40-$1F47 etc.) on even frames OR virtual sprite X+7 (arrays $1F48-$1F4F etc.) on odd frames. sub_929D does the first, sub_92CF the second; both write SP0X+2*(X-1) and SP0Y+2*(X-1).\n\nSo every array here is 16 entries: 0-7 are the frame-0 half and 8-15 the frame-1 half.\n\n  $1F40  sprX      X position\n  $1F50  sprY      Y position\n  $1F60  sprColour SPMC1 value; bit 7 means auto-increment it every frame (sub_929D $92B7), i.e. colour-cycle\n  $1F70  sprShape  sprite pointer, written straight to $07F8+n\n  $1F80  sprInUse  0 = free. sub_92F6 allocates (writes $88), sub_9328 frees.\n\n$1F98/$1F99 are the two $D015 enable masks and $1F9C/$1F9D the two $D010 MSB-of-X masks, one per frame parity. sub_92F6 ORs the new sprite's bit into $1F98 when the slot is 0-7 and into $1F99 when it is 8-15 (it tests bit 3 of the slot number).\n\nEvidence: $92CB STA $07F7,X with X=1..8 writes $07F8-$07FF, which pins the screen at $0400 and the sprite pointers where the hardware expects them." },
        { "id": "cmt_e4hite", "address": "$1FB0", "text": "Per-object-slot byte (indexed by X, same slot numbering as obj_type at $1D10). Bit 7 is tested alone in several places (e.g. sub_9413: AND #$80) as an on/off flag; the low 7 bits, masked with AND #$7F, are doubled and used as the index into obj_behavior_jumptable ($9933) by sub_993A -- so this byte packs a flag and a behaviour selector together. Same AND #$7F / AND #$80 packing shows up on dat_1D60 and dat_1DF8 too -- a recurring one-flag-plus-7-bit-value convention across several of these per-object tables, not something the model here has a bitfield vocabulary for beyond this comment." },
        { "id": "cmt_vhjmsn", "address": "$1FF0", "text": "newColumnChars: the six characters of the next terrain column, staged here before EmitTerrainColumn writes them to screen column 39, rows 14-19. $1FF9 is the colour used for all six.\n\nNote this renders as spriteSet1 + $07Fx wherever it is not named: the label at $1800 carries an extent of 2K that reaches up here, and $1D00-$1FFF is game variables rather than sprite data. The extent is one page too generous." },
        { "id": "cmt_o243tc", "address": "$2000", "text": "The game font, and it is HALF a character set: 128 glyphs at $2000-$23FF, not the 2K the VIC could address from here.\n\n$D018 = $18 at $8AE7 puts the character base at $2000. I rendered it: chunky 8x8 glyphs, A-Z at screen codes $01-$1A, digits at $30-$39, punctuation in $20-$3F. Every text table in the program is screen codes for this font, which is why $52 and $56 turn up mid-word in the messages — they are glyphs this font defines and the standard set does not.\n\nCORRECTED after reading the zone data. I first declared this region 2K, up to $2800. It is not: the zone records use sprite blocks $90-$9F, and those are $90*64 = $2400 to $27FF. Rendering $2400 as sprites gives a walking creature in three frames; rendering it as characters gives nothing. So the upper half of what the VIC would call the character set is sprite data, which is fine because the game never writes a screen code above $7F." },
        { "id": "cmt_hxe8uk", "address": "$2400", "text": "7K of sprite data, blocks $90-$FF, and every zone uses it.\n\nThese are NOT in the bank table at $502F and do not need to be: the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF, so anything from $0800 to $3FFF is reachable by a sprite pointer directly. That is exactly why the table exists — $4000 and $4800 are OUTSIDE the bank, so a set kept there has to be copied down to $0800-$0FFF first.\n\nHow the split works, read off the zone records. Every one of the 42 zones draws its creatures from blocks $20-$3F, which is the SWAPPED bank at $0800-$0FFF, plus blocks in this range, which are always there. So the resident set is the shared vocabulary — explosions at $EC-$EE appear in all 42 zones — and the swapped bank is what makes zone 30 look different from zone 3.\n\nStarts at $2400 rather than $2800: blocks $90-$9F live in what would otherwise be the top half of the character set. Rendered, they are a walking creature." },
        { "id": "cmt_z27m7q", "address": "$3000", "text": "THE PLAYER, and it is a camel — I drew it.\n\nSprite block $C0 lives at $C0*64 = $3000, and the player-control routine at $94AA writes #$C0 into sprShape (virtual sprite 0) when you press left, #$CD at $94CF for fire+up and #$CE at $94E5 for fire+down. Rendering those blocks gives a quadruped with a long neck and a head — a camel, in a standing frame and three leaping ones at $3340.\n\nThat closes three separate chains at once. The player is virtual sprite slot 0 and object slot 0, which is what loc_5049 compares every creature against when it turns them to face you. The shapes are in $2800-$3FFF, addressable by the VIC without copying, which is why the bank-swap table at $502F never mentions them. And it confirms the whole sprite-multiplexer reading, because sprShape really is the byte that ends up at $07F8." },
        { "id": "cmt_4jnc50", "address": "$3B00", "text": "THE EXPLOSION, and it is an ordinary creature.\n\nBlocks $EC, $ED, $EE. Rendered they are three frames of a sparse symmetric dot pattern spreading outward — a starburst.\n\nWhat makes this worth a comment is that every one of the 42 zone records devotes one of its eight CREATURE TYPES to it. Check zone 0: type 3 is animFirst = animLast·s range $EC-$EE, moveType 1, lifetime $0C, tmplNextType $FF. A short-lived object that dies when its time is up. And tmplTypeWhenExploded for the other seven types is 3, 3, 3, 0, 3, 3, 3, 3 — they all turn INTO type 3 when they explode.\n\nSo the game has no explosion subsystem. An explosion is a creature that lives twelve ticks and then dies, and blowing something up is the same operation as a bird laying an egg: sub_9772 with a different type. That is the whole object model paying for itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_88qild", "address": "$4000", "text": "Sprite data, out of VIC reach at $4000, so it is copied to $0800 by $5007 when a wave asks for it. Rendered as hires it comes out dithered #.#. all over, which is the signature of MULTICOLOUR sprite data read one bit per pixel: these are two-bit-per-pixel shapes." },
        { "id": "cmt_ry156z", "address": "$4000", "text": "Confirmed multicolour, and it is the zone 30-35 set. Rendered at two bits per pixel — 12 pixels wide instead of 24, four colours — the shapes resolve cleanly: a fish or rocket, a tall box, a diagonal beam. Rendered as hires the same bytes come out dithered #.#. all over, which is the giveaway.\n\nThe zone records agree: $D025 and $D026, the two shared sprite multicolour registers, are set per zone from offsets +157 and +158, and every zone using this bank sets them to a real pair. The box is plausibly the portaloo of zone 31, SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS." },
        { "id": "cmt_edr1wc", "address": "$4800", "text": "Sprite data, out of VIC reach, copied to $0800 by $5007. Rendered: explosion or effect shapes and some letter-shaped sprites." },
        { "id": "cmt_3yc4gz", "address": "$5007", "text": "CORRECTED (basalt): this copies a SPRITE SET, not a character set, and beryl reached the same conclusion independently. I have renamed it CopySpriteSet.\n\nThe caller puts an even index in $02. spriteSetSourceTable ($502F) is two interleaved byte tables read as LDA $502F,X (low) / LDA $5030,X (high), so X = 0,2,4,6,8,10 selects source $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800. It copies 2048 bytes to $0800-$0FFF ($05 is stepped until it reaches $10, which is what bounds it).\n\nEvidence it is sprites:\n- 2048 bytes at $0800 is 32 sprites of 64, i.e. VIC sprite pointer values $20-$3F.\n- I rendered $1000 and $1800 through run_decoder as 24x21 monochrome sprites and got recognisable creatures at both. $4000 gives dithered multicolour-looking shapes. Not glyphs.\n- The only write to VMCSB in the whole program is $8AE7 STA #$18, which puts the character base at $2000 and leaves it there; AnimateCharacters ($9206) writes character data to $2110 and $2148, confirming $2000. So $0800 is not the charset.\n- Its caller $9CB5 sets SPMC0, SPMC1, XXPAND and YXPAND in the same breath.\n- No instruction anywhere reads or writes $0800-$0FFF absolutely, which is what you expect of data only the VIC looks at.\n\nNOTE this routine reuses $02-$05 as two pointers (source $02/$03, destination $04/$05), not as the PrintCharAt arguments those bytes hold everywhere else in this block.\n\nStill unexplained: the last two table entries are $5000 and $5800, which is this program code. Either those slots are never selected, or the interleaved lo/hi reading is wrong for them." },
        { "id": "cmt_5fooej", "address": "$5007", "text": "Copies 2048 bytes ($0800-$0FFF) from a source selected by bank_copy_ptr_tbl ($502F/$5030), indexed by X = zero page $02 as set by the caller before the JSR. Destination is fixed. Looks like a bank-switcher swapping some 2K asset (font or HUD graphics bank) into a shared work area; see the comment on bank_copy_ptr_tbl for why the exact table layout is not pinned down." },
        { "id": "cmt_jz9hcn", "address": "$5007", "text": "beryl: I think this copies a SPRITE bank, not a character set, and here is the evidence — the existing name says charset and I have left it alone rather than rename over somebody.\n\n- The destination is $0800-$0FFF. Sprite pointers live at $07F8-$07FF (proved at $92CB, STA $07F7,X with X=1..8), and $0800/64 = block $20, so $0800-$0FFF is sprite blocks $20-$3F.\n- The character set is at $2000, not $0800. $8AE7 writes #$18 to $D018: screen $0400 (bits 7-4 = %0001), charbase %100 * $800 = $2000. I rendered $2000 and it is a legible chunky font, A-Z at screen codes $01-$1A and digits at $30-$39.\n- I rendered the sources. $1000 draws as a camel in walk frames at 24x21, three bytes per row, 64 bytes per block. $1800 draws as creatures. $4000 draws as a multicolour sprite set. None of them are 8-byte glyphs.\n\nUnresolved: the table at $502F holds SIX pointers - $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800 - and the last two are this code and the text below it. Either indices 8 and 10 are never passed, or something loads data over $5000 after this point. The wave descriptor table, wherever it is, is what would settle it." },
        { "id": "cmt_ix35tf", "address": "$502F", "text": "beryl: SIX entries, and only FOUR are reachable. Settled by reading the data rather than the code.\n\n  index 0 -> $1000    zones 0-25\n  index 2 -> $1800    zones 26-29\n  index 4 -> $4000    zones 30-35\n  index 6 -> $4800    zones 36-41\n  index 8 -> $5000    never selected\n  index 10 -> $5800   never selected\n\nThe index comes from offset +156 of the zone record, and I decoded all 42 records: the value is 0, 2, 4 or 6 and never 8 or 10. So the last two pointers are dead entries left in the table, which is a relief — they point at this code and at the text below it.\n\nWorth noting what the four live ones say about the game: the sprite bank changes exactly three times in 42 zones, so the creatures you meet in the last six are drawn from a set nobody sees for the first thirty-six." },
        { "id": "cmt_mvqzr7", "address": "$502F", "text": "Read by sub_5007 (LDX from zp $02, then LDA dat_502F,X / LDA dat_5030,X to build a source pointer, then copies 2048 bytes to $0800-$0FFF). Only 1 byte of dat_502F and 11 of dat_5030 are visible in this gap; if read as (lo=dat_502F[X], hi=dat_5030[X]) with X stepping by 1, dat_5030 bytes 10,00,18,00,40,00,48,00,50,00,58 look like six little-endian 16-bit source addresses ($0010,$0018,$0040,$0048,$0050,$0058) rather than six separate lo/hi pairs looked up by a doubled index -- but this is read from the shape of the copy loop, not confirmed by seeing a caller set X, so treat the exact indexing scheme as unresolved." },
        { "id": "cmt_r0dizf", "address": "$503B", "text": "Move type: run AWAY from the direction the player last fired. It is SetSpeedFromFireDirection ($9F02) called with bulletDirection ($3B) complemented - EOR #$FF - and the original value put back afterwards, so the shared routine is reused rather than duplicated with the signs flipped.\n\nReached by JMP from $9CAA in the move-type dispatch, so it is a sibling of the $9F02 case rather than a caller of it." },
        { "id": "cmt_x4ijp0", "address": "$5049", "text": "HOME TOWARDS THE PLAYER. X is the object slot; slot 0 is the player, which is why every comparison is against the un-indexed element of the same array.\n\n  objXPos,X >= objXPos[0]  ->  set bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX  (move left, towards the player)\n  objXPos,X <  objXPos[0]  ->  clear it                    (move right)\nand the same for Y through objBaseSpeedY. The result is written to both the base speed and the live speed, so it takes effect on the next move without waiting for a respawn.\n\nBit 7 of a speed byte is its sign - the same convention as $9F02, which writes $81 for negative one and $01 for positive - so this is not a flag being set, it is the sign being chosen. Nothing here changes the magnitude, so a homing creature keeps whatever speed its zone gave it and only ever turns." },
        { "id": "cmt_i5wqi0", "address": "$50D9", "text": "\"BEASTS REMAINING: n\" on messageLine ($0590, screen row 10).\nThe digit is written at $05AD as '0' + beastsRemaining ($54). If the sum is still '0' - i.e. no beasts left - it calls ShowBeastsExtinct ($53EC) which overwrites the line with \"YOUR BEASTS ARE EXTINCT, PLAYER ONE\" (\"TWO\" patched in at $05B2 when playerState is 2). So $54 is the life count and this is the between-lives screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_sbeufl", "address": "$511B", "text": "HUD line, decodes (screen-code) to a string ending in BEASTS REMAINING, one non-letter glyph byte, then a trailing 0. The trailing 0 is presumably overwritten at runtime with the live beast count." },
        { "id": "cmt_s5lnrq", "address": "$5199", "text": "HUD line, decodes to STAND BY YOUR BEAST, one non-letter glyph byte, then PLAYER ONE. That same glyph code is reused in msg_beasts_extinct and msg_enter_your_name below. The trailing ONE is patched to TWO for player 2 by suffix_two_patch_a, copied by sub_5143." },
        { "id": "cmt_qa2b5b", "address": "$51C0", "text": "Copied by sub_5143 (LDX#3; loop LDA suffix_two_patch_a,X / STA dat_05AE,X / DEX / BNE) over the tail of msg_stand_by_player's copy in the work buffer when zp location cur_player == 2. Only bytes 1-3 (TWO) are actually written; byte 0 (a space) is never copied since the loop stops at X==0." },
        { "id": "cmt_295ilj", "address": "$51E8", "text": "THE OPTIONS SCREEN. Reached from CheckOptionsKey ($52EE), which fires on LSTX $26 = the 'O' key.\n\nDraws, via PrintCharAt ($883A):\n  row 3 col 15  \"OPTIONS\"\n  row 6 col 9   \"WAVE SEQUENCY: NORMAL\"   ($52E8 \"RANDOM\" overwrites col 24 when waveSequencyRandom is set)\n  row 8 col 14  \"PLAYERS: 1\"              ('2' written at col 23 when playerState is non-zero)\n  ShowZoneDistance ($5300) adds row 10 col 8 \"DISTANCE BETWEEN ZONES: nn\"\n\nThen polls LSTX. The four codes it tests are the function keys in the C64 keyboard matrix:\n  $04 = F1  toggle waveSequencyRandom ($59, EOR #$FF)\n  $05 = F3  playerState ($56) = (playerState+1) AND 1  -> 1 or 2 players\n  $06 = F5  zoneDistance ($57) += 2, wrapping $15 back to 7\n  $03 = F7  leave: PLA/PLA to drop the caller's return address, JMP $8AEA\n\nThe PLA/PLA at $52B6 means this never returns to whoever called it - it exits straight into the game at $8AEA." },
        { "id": "cmt_3f0jje", "address": "$51EB", "text": "Options-menu input loop. F1 (matrix 4) toggles RandomZoneModeFlag $59. F3 (matrix 5) toggles a second binary flag $56 (0/1) - not yet identified, candidate: number-of-players or difficulty. F5 (matrix 6) steps $57 by 2, wrapping 21 back to 7 - a numeric setting, effect not yet traced. F7 (matrix 3) exits back into the game (JMP loc_8AEA, discarding two return addresses off the stack the way $985E does for spawn-cancel)." },
        { "id": "cmt_4jfgge", "address": "$52C2", "text": "Section header on the title/options screen: OPTIONS" },
        { "id": "cmt_eqipob", "address": "$52C9", "text": "decodes to WAVE SEQUENCY then a non-letter glyph byte then NORMAL. Plotted unconditionally by sub_5216ish; the glyph is the same kind of decorative byte seen elsewhere (values above the A-Z/space/digit range)." },
        { "id": "cmt_w5ouro", "address": "$52DE", "text": "decodes to PLAYERS then a glyph then 1. Only plotted (at $525B onward) when zero page $59 is set -- a second options row shown conditionally." },
        { "id": "cmt_bp6ght", "address": "$52E8", "text": "decodes to RANDOM. Companion label to opt_players_1, same conditional block." },
        { "id": "cmt_hmxjwh", "address": "$533F", "text": "decodes to DISTANCE BETWEEN ZONES then a glyph then 00. The trailing two-digit field is a BCD-ish counter at dat_05B0/dat_05B1, stepped by 2 (wrapping 0-20 back to 7) by sub_5300 when key scancode 6 (LSTX==6) is pressed on the options screen -- this is the difficulty/spacing setting, zero page $57 holds the raw value that dat_05B0/05B1 render as decimal." },
        { "id": "cmt_ukqp0k", "address": "$539B", "text": "SWAP PLAYERS. playerState ($56) is a three-value thing, and this is where that shows:\n  0 = one-player game\n  1 = two-player game, player one up\n  2 = two-player game, player two up\n\n$56 = 0 -> save and restore the same slot, so the swap is a no-op.\n$56 = 1 -> becomes 2, then falls into the save-to-slot-1 path.\n$56 = 2 -> becomes 1, saves to slot 2.\n\nA slot is 40 bytes of the status line ($5F00 for player one, $5F80 for player two) plus three bytes of per-player state: $58, $50 and beastsRemaining ($54), kept at $5F30-$5F32 and $5FB0-$5FB2. LoadPlayerStatusLine ($5359) is the other half.\n\nThe options screen toggles $56 between 0 and 1 only; the value 2 is only ever produced here." },
        { "id": "cmt_01vsh3", "address": "$540C", "text": "HUD line, decodes to YOUR BEASTS ARE EXTINCT, the same non-letter glyph byte, then PLAYER ONE. Same ONE/TWO patch mechanism via suffix_two_patch_b." },
        { "id": "cmt_98u7zj", "address": "$5434", "text": "Same mechanism as suffix_two_patch_a at $51C0, duplicated here for sub_53EC's copy of msg_beasts_extinct instead of msg_stand_by_player." },
        { "id": "cmt_ke197c", "address": "$545C", "text": "Nothing calls this in the code reached so far, hence the missing root -- but it is unambiguous: LDX#0/LDA#$20/STA $5E00,X/INX/BNE fills all 256 bytes of $5E00-$5EFF with spaces, then LDA highscore_entry_1,X / AND #$3F / STA $5E00,X for X=0..207 copies the 208-byte default high-score table (highscore_entry_1..4 at $5474) into it, converting each byte from PETSCII/ASCII (the table encoding, letters 0x41-0x5A) to screen-code (0x01-0x1A) by masking the top two bits. This confirms, rather than just size-matches, that $5474 is the seed data for the $5E00 buffer the C0xx routines LOAD/SAVE as ATTACK MUTANT.HI." },
        { "id": "cmt_qh71pe", "address": "$545C", "text": "Rebuild highScoreTable ($5E00) from defaultHighScoreTable ($5474) - the fallback when the .HI file did not load.\nFills $5E00-$5EFF with spaces, then copies $D0 = 208 bytes of ASCII masked AND #$3F.\n\nNothing in the analysis reaches this address: it was 27 bytes of unexplained data until I declared it a function (+12 instructions). Its caller is presumably in the loader path around $C065/$8A3C, reached by a route the static walk does not follow." },
        { "id": "cmt_dya1jf", "address": "$5474", "text": "Default/shipped high-score table, 4 entries x 52 bytes = 208 bytes, the same size as the $5E00 buffer the C0xx routines LOAD/SAVE as ATTACK MUTANT.HI -- almost certainly the seed content for that file before any score is ever saved. Message: I LIKE CAMELS REALLY" },
        { "id": "cmt_zgf3v3", "address": "$5474", "text": "The built-in high score table: four entries of 52 bytes, rank digit + 7 score digits + name.\n  1  0000000    I LIKE CAMELS REALLY\n  2  0000000    I GOT NOTHING AGAINST SHEEP EITHER\n  3  0000000    LLAMAS ARE LLOVELY\n  4  0000000    2D SPACE INVADERS RULE THE ZONE OK\nStored as uppercase ASCII and masked AND #$3F on the way to the screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_g9550s", "address": "$54A8", "text": "Second slot of the default high-score table. Message: I GOT NOTHING AGAINST SHEEP EITHER" },
        { "id": "cmt_64bg9j", "address": "$54DC", "text": "Third slot of the default high-score table. Message: LLAMAS ARE LLOVELY" },
        { "id": "cmt_ru6dow", "address": "$5510", "text": "Fourth slot of the default high-score table. Message: 2D SPACE INVADERS RULE THE ZONE OK" },
        { "id": "cmt_nnpgw6", "address": "$5544", "text": "HIGH SCORE CHECK, run at end of game.\n\nhighScoreTable ($5E00) is 4 entries of $34 = 52 bytes. Entry n starts at $5E00 + n*$34. Byte 0 is the rank digit '1'..'4'; bytes 1..7 are the 7 score digits; the rest is the name text. That layout is confirmed three ways: the $34 stride here, InitDefaultHighScores ($545C) copying $D0 = 4*$34 bytes, and the renumbering loop at $567F which writes '1'..'4' at $5E00, $5E34, $5E68, $5E9C.\n\nThe comparison walks Y = 1..7 comparing scoreField+Y ($040E-$0414, the score on the status line) against ($60),Y for each of the four entries in turn. Higher than an entry -> $556E inserts: the table below is shifted down $34 bytes ($5587 copies backwards until it hits a $FF sentinel), the new score is written in, and name entry begins at $55AE.\n\nThe table is loaded from disk at $C065 and saved back at $C082 (amber's finding); InitDefaultHighScores supplies it when the file is not there." },
        { "id": "cmt_69rxl8", "address": "$55ED", "text": "HUD line, decodes to ENTER YOUR NAME, the same glyph byte, then PLAYER, then ONE at offsets 24-26 and TWO at offsets 27-29 back to back. Unlike the two messages above, this one bakes both suffixes into the same table; the caller at loc_561E picks which 3-char window to read by adding 3 to the starting index when cur_player==2, instead of patching after the copy." },
        { "id": "cmt_oj0yjk", "address": "$5695", "text": "Block until a key is pressed, then return its game character code.\nWaits for LSTX ($C5) to leave $40 (= no key), then translates through keyMatrixCodeTable/keyResultTable. An unrecognised key is ignored and the wait restarts. Returns $00 for INS/DEL (backspace) and $FF for RETURN." },
        { "id": "cmt_cpplod", "address": "$56AE", "text": "Values 0-1A (screen-code A-Z), then space, two more codes, then FF as a sentinel. Read by sub_5695 as kbd_char_table,X after X is found by scanning kbd_scan_table for the current LSTX value -- X is a shared index into both tables; this one gives the character to return." },
        { "id": "cmt_hf3ytu", "address": "$56AE", "text": "KEY DECODE TABLES, and the proof that this game uses a CUSTOM CHARACTER SET.\n\nReadKeyMapped ($5695) searches keyMatrixCodeTable ($56CD) for the value in LSTX ($C5) and returns the byte at the same index in keyResultTable ($56AE). Lining the two tables up against the standard C64 keyboard matrix scan codes gives an exact, unambiguous mapping:\n\n  index  result  matrix code  key\n      0    $00        $00     INS/DEL   (handled as backspace at $5645)\n      1    $01        $0A     A\n      2    $02        $1C     B\n    ...   ...        ...      ... straight through to\n     26    $1A        $0C     Z\n     27    $20        $3C     SPACE\n     28    $51        $2C     . (full stop)\n     29    $52        $2F     , (comma)\n     30    $FF        $01     RETURN     (terminates name entry, $5659)\n\nEvery one of the 26 letters lines up. That pins two glyphs of the game's own charset:\n  $51 = '.'   $52 = ','\nwhich are NOT the standard screen codes ($2E and $2C). Two more follow from context in the message text:\n  $54 = '!'   ($57A7 \"WELL DONE! ... CAMELKIND!\")\n  $56 = ':'   ($52C9 \"WAVE SEQUENCY: NORMAL\", $52DE \"PLAYERS: 1\", $533F \"DISTANCE BETWEEN ZONES: 00\")\n\nSo the built-in screen/petscii decoders are correct for A-Z and space in this project and WRONG for punctuation. The charset itself is one of the blocks CopyCharSetToRAM ($5007) moves to $0800." },
        { "id": "cmt_svv2qy", "address": "$56AE", "text": "Two more glyphs, found later in the wave banners at $6700 and the scroll text at $5A00:\n  $53 = apostrophe   (\"HAVEN'T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE\", \"ROCK 'N' ROLLUPS\", \"WELCOME TO 'REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS'\")\n  $55 = question mark (\"WHAT IS A BRAHM, ANYWAY?\", \"MORE TEA, VICAR?\")\nTogether with $51, $52, $54, $56 from the key tables above, the game's punctuation occupies a contiguous run:\n  $51 .   $52 ,   $53 '   $54 !   $55 ?   $56 :\nwhich is a deliberate layout rather than six coincidences, and is why I trust the two that were read off context rather than off the key matrix." },
        { "id": "cmt_7u8hyt", "address": "$56CD", "text": "31 raw CIA keyboard scan codes (from LSTX / $C5), same length and same index space as kbd_char_table at $56AE. sub_5695 loops X 0..31 comparing LSTX against this table; a match at position X yields kbd_char_table[X] as the typed character. This is the keyboard-scancode-to-screen-code relation behind the name-entry screen (see msg_enter_your_name)." },
        { "id": "cmt_keuxwy", "address": "$56EC", "text": "TEXT SCROLLED INTO THE TERRAIN. Called from $8CAB once per new landscape column, and this is what puts the credits, the high score table and \"HERE WE GO\" into the scenery rather than onto a text line - which is why none of that text is drawn by PrintCharAt anywhere.\n\nIt fills the seven characters of the new column ($1FF0 newColumnChars) with spaces and then plants one or two characters into it.\n\nattractScrollState ($5A) is a mode byte with a counter in the low nibble:\n  $00        off - $8CA9 skips this routine entirely\n  $01-$0F    attract mode. Column row 3 gets highScoreTable[scrollIndex] and row 5 gets\n             attractScrollText[scrollIndex] through scrollTextPtr, colour 1 (white). scrollIndex\n             ($5B) advances one per column; when it wraps past $FF the pointer high byte steps,\n             and when that reaches $5E - the end of the credits text - attractScrollState is\n             zeroed and the scroll stops.\n  $10-$1F    \"HERE WE GO\" ($5756), one character per column, rows 3 and 5, colour 4. The low\n             nibble indexes the string; INC then AND #$1F means after 16 columns it becomes $00\n             and the routine switches itself off.\n\nSet to $03 at $8AAF when the program starts (attract mode) and to $10 at $8FA6, immediately before ShowStandByYourBeast - so \"HERE WE GO\" scrolls past you as a life begins.\n\nTwo streams share one index: the high score table and the credits text are both walked by scrollIndex, so during attract the landscape carries both at once, on different rows." },
        { "id": "cmt_6kzu68", "address": "$5756", "text": "decodes to HERE WE GO then three copies of the same non-letter glyph byte (0x51) then padding -- likely three exclamation-style decorative glyphs in this custom charset." },
        { "id": "cmt_t4welj", "address": "$57A7", "text": "decodes to WELL DONE then a glyph then YOU HAVE SAVED CAMELKIND then the same glyph again then padding -- end-of-game congratulation message. The glyph (0x54) is yet another of the non-letter decorative bytes seen throughout this file (0x51/0x52/0x54/0x56 so far), consistent with punctuation drawn from a redefined character set rather than the standard A-Z range." },
        { "id": "cmt_j2ogdb", "address": "$57D1", "text": "Called unconditionally at the end of ClearMessageLine (sub_9406). If cheat flag $5E is zero, just RTS (message line stays blank). If set, redraws the 40-column message line with CheatModeBannerText in colour $01. So every ordinary message-line clear during play doubles as the cheat banner refresh once the code is entered." },
        { "id": "cmt_wcwnqk", "address": "$57D1", "text": "Draws \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" on messageLine when cheatFlag ($5E) is set, and sets $4F = 7.\nLike the other ASCII-stored strings in this block ($5474, $57EF, $5848, $90F8) the text is held as plain uppercase ASCII/PETSCII and converted to screen codes at draw time with AND #$3F. The screen-code strings ($511B, $5199, $52C2...) are stored already converted. Both kinds are in this one block, which is worth knowing before declaring an encoding." },
        { "id": "cmt_e9mvvq", "address": "$57EF", "text": "decodes to CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE, space padded both sides." },
        { "id": "cmt_zkyk1h", "address": "$57EF", "text": "Screen-code text \"  CHEAT  MODE   OPERATIVE  \" padded to 40 chars, poked into the message line ($0590) with colour $01 (white) when the cheat flag $5E is set." },
        { "id": "cmt_8a2tjb", "address": "$5817", "text": "PAUSE. Any key down (LSTX not $40) puts \"PAUSE\" on messageLine and holds until the key is released and another is pressed. Returns immediately when cheatFlag ($5E) is set, so cheat mode disables pause." },
        { "id": "cmt_1m88ke", "address": "$5848", "text": "HUD line: PAUSE MODE ON, then space padding, then ANY KEY RESTARTS." },
        { "id": "cmt_1fkbjy", "address": "$5870", "text": "ASSEMBLER SOURCE LEFT IN THE SHIPPED BINARY. Not data the game uses - residue from the machine it was built on, saved into the image because this range was never cleared.\n\nIt is stored the way a Commodore assembler that lives inside BASIC stores it: a chain of [link lo][link hi][line number lo][line number hi] text $00. Following the links from $5877 gives line numbers 13880 to 13990 in steps of ten, which is what proves the format rather than a coincidence of printable bytes:\n\n  13880   STA $07F7,X\n  13890   RTS\n  13900  SSET TYA\n  13910   PHA\n  13920   AND #$07\n  13930   SEC\n  13940   TAY\n  13950   INY\n  13960   LDA #$00\n  13970  TANGE ROL A\n  13980   DEY\n  13990   BNE TANGE (the operand runs off the end of the salvaged text)\n\nSSET/TANGE is a bit-mask builder: take a sprite number 0-7, shift a 1 left that many places. STA $07F7,X with X = 1..8 writes $07F8-$07FF, the sprite shape pointers for a screen at $0400 - which is where this game puts the screen. So these eleven lines are a sprite-enable and shape-set routine from the original source, and the author called the mask loop TANGE and its entry SSET.\n\nThe chain breaks at $5906 because the next link points at $3202, outside this block. There are two more fragments at $5F28 and $5FA8." },
        { "id": "cmt_b5227c", "address": "$5870", "text": "BASIC-style tokenized-listing text sitting in the middle of otherwise-unexplained data: fragments read (via read_bytes, plain ASCII) as \"...STA $07F7,X\" / RTS / \"SSET TYA\" / PHA / \"AND #$07\" / SEC / TAY / INY / \"LDA #$00\" / \"TANGE ROL A\". Checked against the live disassembly at $92F6: TYA/PHA/AND#$07/SEC/TAY/INY/LDA#$00/ROL A/DEY/BNE -- byte-for-byte identical, instruction for instruction. So $92F6 really is what the author called SSET and $92FF is TANGE. This is the authors own source buffer, saved as part of the memory image." },
        { "id": "cmt_ejwnez", "address": "$5870", "placement": "after", "text": "Closer look: this repeats a small record shape roughly every 8-9 bytes -- a $00 terminator, two mystery bytes, a literal X, two more mystery bytes, a literal 6, a space, then a short mnemonic-shaped ASCII fragment (STA/RTS/TYA/PHA/AND/SEC/TAY/INY/LDA/ROL/DEY/BNE) with no operand. Reads like entries of the form (something, something, label-text) rather than raw code or a plain message. Not chased further -- would need to find what reads this table to know what the two mystery-byte fields are." },
        { "id": "cmt_gcmcov", "address": "$5870", "text": "Leftover assembler source, BASIC line format. See the comment at $5870." },
        { "id": "cmt_lf8izd", "address": "$5870", "text": "Not understood. Read as plain ASCII/PETSCII it contains recognisable 6502-mnemonic-shaped fragments -- STA $07F7,X / RTS / TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 / ROL A / DEY / BNE -- interleaved with bytes outside printable range, so it is neither clean text nor anything this reading could place a shape on. Recorded as a plain data span rather than guessed at further; flagging this honestly instead of inventing a structure for it." },
        { "id": "cmt_yyuxj5", "address": "$5870", "text": "NOT GAME DATA. This is leftover text from the machine the game was written on: 6502 source lines held in BASIC line format - 2-byte link, 2-byte line number, then the text, then $00. The links point at $5878, $5888, $5891, $589E ... which is consistent with this having been resident at these addresses, and the line numbers run 13880, 13890, 13900, ... in steps of 10.\n\nWhat survives here reads:\n   STA $07F7,X / RTS / SSET TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 /\n   TANGE ROL A / DEY / BNE ...\n\nMore of the same is at $5F26-$5FFF, underneath the player status save slots, and that fragment carries the author's own routine names:\n   ; CLC / ; ADC #$C8 / ; STA NEXINI / ; LDA NEXINI+1 / ; ADC #$00\n   ONZER JM... / NI1 ; READ DATA TABLE\n   MLOOP JSR STARF / JSR BULITZ / JSR CCAMEL\n\nSo the main loop was called MLOOP and it called STARF, BULITZ and CCAMEL - starfield, bullets and camel. ADC #$C8 next to NEXINI is the same $C8 stride as the wave table at $6700, which is a second, independent confirmation of that record size.\n\nThe game overwrites all of this at runtime: $5E00 becomes the high score table and $5F00/$5F80 the two player status slots. It is residue, not code, and nothing reads it." },
        { "id": "cmt_649zc2", "address": "$5900", "text": "basalt: two-voice jingle, $5900-$594B and $5980-$59A7, in the same (ticks, note) format as the intro tune at $6000 and read by the same player. StartTune ($5768) seeds $13/$14 = $58FE and $15/$16 = $597E - two below each, because the player advances the pointer before reading.\n\nCallers: $55B1 (just before high-score name entry) and $579C (ShowWellDone), which spins on tunePlaying ($1A) until it finishes. So this is the short end-of-game jingle, not the intro.\n\nRenamed from tuneVoice1/tuneVoice2 to shortTuneVoice1/shortTuneVoice2 to keep them apart from the three-voice intro tune." },
        { "id": "cmt_7jrhhz", "address": "$5900", "text": "Two short tables of small paired bytes (roughly duration/value pairs), each ending in F0 FF 00 FF, then zero padding out to $5A00. Shape consistent with a short two-voice tune or sound-effect script (e.g. for the pause jingle heard nearby), but the exact field meaning was not traced into the SID-writing code that would confirm it." },
        { "id": "cmt_udk2qo", "address": "$5900", "text": "INTRO MUSIC, two voices, as (duration, note) byte pairs terminated by $F0 $FF $00 $FF.\n  tuneVoice1 $5900-$594B (38 pairs)\n  tuneVoice2 $5980-$59A7 (20 pairs)\n\nStartTune ($5768) points the players at them: $13/$14 = $58FE and $15/$16 = $597E, i.e. each start address minus 2, so the player pre-increments by 2 before its first read. It also sets tunePlaying ($1A) = 1; ShowWellDone ($5787) spins on that byte until the tune finishes.\n\nThe credits text calls this \"THE INTRODUCTORY MUSIC ... ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY JAMES LISNEY\". I have not found the player itself, so which byte of a pair is pitch and which is length is inference from the shape (the second of each pair moves over a small range, the first is mostly 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, $0D)." },
        { "id": "cmt_q29r15", "address": "$5A00", "text": "THE ATTRACT-MODE SCROLL TEXT, 1024 bytes of screen codes, $5A00-$5DFF.\n\nBase proved at $8A96: LDA #$00 / STA $5C, LDA #$5A / STA $5D, so scrollTextPtr = $5A00. The scroller at $56EC walks it with ($5C),Y and stops when the high byte reaches $5E ($571B CMP #$5E), which is exactly where the high score table begins - so the text is bounded by $5DFF, and the tail is padded with spaces.\n\nIt reads (with the game charset: $51=. $52=, $53=apostrophe $54=! $55=? $56=:):\n\n  WELCOME TO 'REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS'. THIS PROGRAM WAS WRITTEN OVER A TWO\n  MONTH PERIOD BY JEFF MINTER. THE INTRODUCTORY MUSIC WAS ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY\n  JAMES LISNEY. THE GAME CONSISTS OF FORTY TWO DIFFERENT ATTACK WAVES. TWO MODES\n  OF PLAY ARE AVAILABLE: NORMAL WAVE SEQUENCY, STARTING AT WAVE 1 AND PROCEEDING\n  TO LEVEL 42, OR RANDOM WAVE SEQUENCY, WHERE YOUR NEXT WAVE IS RANDOMLY DRAWN\n  FROM ANY OF THE FIRST 32. PRESS 'O' AND USE THE FKEYS TO CONTROL THE OPTIONS.\n  ... SPECIAL THANKS ... UNLESS YOU COPIED IT, IN WHICH CASE MAY THE FLEAS OF\n  1000 CAMELS INFEST YOUR ARMPITS FOREVER.\n\nThat text is a primary source for the rest of the project: 42 waves (the wave table at $6700 has exactly 42 records), the two wave-sequency modes (waveSequencyRandom, $59), the random mode drawing from the first 32, and 'O' opening the options screen (CheckOptionsKey at $52EE tests LSTX $26, which is the O key)." },
        { "id": "cmt_tsxwhf", "address": "$5A00", "text": "Long scrolling text block, ending exactly at $5E00 (the high-score save buffer). Opens WELCOME TO REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS / THIS PROGRAM WAS WRITTEN OVER A TWO MONTH PERIOD BY JEFF MINTER / THE INTRODUCTORY MUSIC WAS ARRANGED FOR TH[E...] and continues from there -- this confirms the game title and author from the bytes themselves. I already knew both facts about this real game before this reading; recording that here rather than presenting it as a bytes-only discovery. Not transcribed further by hand past the opening lines; 1024 bytes is a lot of scrolling prose and the point of this claim is coverage, not a full transcript." },
        { "id": "cmt_0dqu67", "address": "$5E00", "text": "THE IMAGE ALREADY CONTAINS A BUILT TABLE, and comparing it with the default proves how it got there.\n\nAll 208 bytes here are exactly defaultHighScoreTable at $5474 with each byte ANDed with $3F: 119 bytes identical (spaces and digits, which are the same in PETSCII and screen codes) and the other 89 differing by precisely that mask, none differing any other way. That is what InitDefaultHighScores at $545C does when it copies.\n\nSo this is not fresh RAM and it is not what LOAD put here. It is the result of $545C having been RUN on the machine the build was saved from - the same story as the assembler source at $5870 and $5F28. The shipped image is a memory dump of a development session, not a clean link.\n\nStructure, from the default: four entries of 52 bytes. \"n  0000000    \" is 14 characters - rank, two spaces, seven score digits, four spaces - then 38 characters of name. 4 x 52 = 208, which is exactly the $D0 that $C082 saves and $C065 loads.\n\nThe names are Jeff Minter placeholder jokes: I LIKE CAMELS REALLY, I GOT NOTHING AGAINST SHEEP EITHER, LLAMAS ARE LLOVELY, 2D SPACE INVADERS RULE THE ZONE OK. They are stored in the code as PETSCII and on screen as screen codes, which is why the file on disk holds screen codes and would look like control characters to anything else reading it." },
        { "id": "cmt_4wrcf1", "address": "$5E00", "text": "Live high-score slot 1 in the LOAD/SAVE buffer -- this is the one C0xx actually persists. Confirmed 52 bytes/entry, same layout as HighScoreEntry at $5474 but screen-code encoded (see seed_highscore_buffer at $545C)." },
        { "id": "cmt_mw76kk", "address": "$5E34", "text": "Live high-score slot 2." },
        { "id": "cmt_u44env", "address": "$5E68", "text": "Live high-score slot 3." },
        { "id": "cmt_eva9bk", "address": "$5E9C", "text": "Live high-score slot 4." },
        { "id": "cmt_kybwwg", "address": "$5ED0", "text": "48 spaces. The high score table proper is $5E00-$5ECF (4 x $34); this is padding up to the player status save slots at $5F00 and $5F80. The whole $5E00 page is what $C065 loads from disk and $545C fills with defaults, which is why the padding is part of the file." },
        { "id": "cmt_xsovwb", "address": "$5F00", "text": "Per-player save area, 128 bytes. Selected (alongside player2_state at $5F80) by cur_player in sub_5359/sub_539B, which swap it with the live screen row + zero page $58/$50/$54 so a split-screen two-player game can keep each side status separate. Seeded at cold start ($8A3C) from dat_90F8 for the first 40 bytes." },
        { "id": "cmt_c3oa9h", "address": "$5F28", "text": "More assembler source residue, same [link][line number]text$00 format as $5870. Lines 15150-15200:\n\n  15150   CLC\n  15160   ADC #$C8\n  15170   STA NEXINI\n  15180   LDA NEXINI+1\n  15190   ADC #$00\n\nAdding 200 to a 16-bit pointer the author called NEXINI, and 200 is the size of the on-screen play area in rows or the stride of a table. The plain text just above at $5F26 reads \"   LDA NEX\", the tail of the line before this fragment.\n\nThese bytes are overwritten at runtime: $5F00-$5F27 is the player one status line, so the game writes over the start of this block on the first frame. It survives in the image only because it was in memory when the build was saved." },
        { "id": "cmt_e7w6cs", "address": "$5F28", "text": "Leftover assembler source. Overwritten at runtime by player one status line, which only reaches $5F27." },
        { "id": "cmt_07w542", "address": "$5F80", "text": "Same shape as player1_state at $5F00. First 40 bytes seeded at cold start ($8A3C) from the same source (dat_90F8) as player1_state; a separate 4-byte field at +8 (offset $5F88) is seeded from dat_8A38 by the loop right after." },
        { "id": "cmt_igdfm2", "address": "$5FA8", "text": "The third and best fragment of leftover assembler source. Lines 15240-15260:\n\n  15240  MLOOP JSR STARF\n  15250        JSR BULITZ\n  15260        JSR CCAMEL\n\nThat is the game main loop in the author own names: STARF is the starfield, BULITZ the bullets, CCAMEL the camels. Just above, at $5FA6 and $5FB3, are the tails of two more lines: \"  ONZER JM\" and \"NI1 ; READ DATA TABLE\" - so there is a label ONZER (zone-related) and a commented-out READ DATA TABLE.\n\nA caution about using these: they are names from SOME build, not necessarily this one, and the fragments are eight lines out of a program of thousands. They are evidence about intent, not an index. Nothing here has been renamed on their authority." },
        { "id": "cmt_tc81k0", "address": "$5FA8", "text": "Leftover assembler source: MLOOP JSR STARF / JSR BULITZ / JSR CCAMEL." },
        { "id": "cmt_h6eex2", "address": "$5FFE", "text": "Pointer 13/14 is set to exactly 5FFE by sub_9170 before the sequencer at loc_88F7 runs. Verified (duration,note) pairs are plausible (duration under 20 hex, note in 20-45 hex or note=F0) up to offset 558 (622D); the next two bytes (80 F0) break that pattern and are followed by a run of 16 FF bytes -- likely a second tracks silence or header rather than more of this one, so extent stops there rather than guessing further. sub_9170 also sets a second pointer pair 15/16 = 631E, which read_bytes shows starting a similar-looking but not yet verified (duration,note) stream -- probably a second music track (title vs in-game vs game-over is a common multi-tune pattern for this kind of game) not yet fully bounded." },
        { "id": "cmt_53hbkw", "address": "$6000", "text": "THE TUNE, measured rather than guessed. Summing the duration byte of every pair up to the $FF terminator:\n\n  voice 1  $6000-$6231  281 pairs  636 ticks  23 distinct notes, indices 33-64\n  voice 2  $6320-$64B3  202 pairs  636 ticks  19 distinct notes, indices 33-63\n  voice 3  $65A0-$66F5  171 pairs  604 ticks  15 distinct notes, indices 19-43\n\nVoices 1 and 2 come to EXACTLY the same total, which is what a three-part arrangement of one piece looks like and would not happen by accident. Voice 3 is 604 and then rests, so it is the bass line and it stops before the end. The first 38 pairs of all three voices are byte-identical to voice 1, so the piece opens in unison and splits.\n\nSpeed: the player runs once per call of CommitSprites, which on the game path is once a frame, and the tempo divider $10/$11 is 7. So a tick is about 0.14 s and the whole tune is around 90 seconds.\n\nThe note table it indexes is a true equal-tempered scale - adjacent entries are in ratio 1.0595 and index 12 is exactly twice index 0, 536 against 1072 - but it is not tuned to concert pitch. Index 0 is 536 * 985248 / 16777216 = 31.5 Hz, which sits between B0 at 30.87 and C1 at 32.70, so the whole table is about a third of a semitone sharp of B or two thirds flat of C. Calling any note here by a letter name means choosing which." },
        { "id": "cmt_6rz53f", "address": "$6000", "text": "First stream of the 3-part title-screen arrangement (melody). Pointer $13/$14 seeded to $5FFE by sub_9170 (pre-increments to $6000)." },
        { "id": "cmt_xztoeu", "address": "$6000", "text": "THE INTRO TUNE, three voices, $6000-$66F5. Same (ticks, note) pair format as the player at $88F7/$91BA reads; a pair whose note byte is $FF ends the tune, $F0 is a rest.\n\nStartMusic ($9170) seeds the three pointers two below the data, so:\n  voice 1  $6000-$622F   560 bytes, terminator pair $6230, $FF padding to $623F\n  voice 2  $6320-$64B1   402 bytes, terminator pair $64B2\n  voice 3  $65A0-$66F3   340 bytes, terminator pair $66F4\n\nThis is the music the credits text calls \"ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY JAMES LISNEY\". The three streams begin with identical bytes (02 24 02 2B 02 2B 02 2B 01 29 01 28 01 26 01 29), which is what an arrangement in canon looks like.\n\nThe char animation frames at $6240-$62FF sit between voice 1 and voice 2, and $6300-$631F is zero/$FF padding." },
        { "id": "cmt_f31mjj", "address": "$6320", "text": "Reachable (tick,note) stream for voice 2 of the event-triggered short tune. Seeded by sub_9170 (pointer $15/$16 = $631E, pre-incremented by 2 before first read, so playback truly starts at $6320). 202 pairs, 891 ticks of duration (~17.8s at 50 ticks/sec), ends with an $FF terminator at $64B2. Table decoded and verified with read_bytes + a small script, not by eye." },
        { "id": "cmt_6eye6x", "address": "$64BA", "text": "CORRECTION (beryl, after ed spotted this by decoding the streams independently): this is NOT a cut arrangement or a fourth voice. It is a byte-for-byte duplicate of $63DA-$64B1, the last 216 bytes of the real, reachable harmony voice (IntroVoice2Harmony, $6320-$64B1). Confirmed with read_bytes: all 216 bytes identical, position for position. Preceded by 7 bytes of $FF padding ($64B2-$64B8) plus one stray $30 at $64B9 between the end of the real stream and this duplicate. Still unreachable (no pointer anywhere in the image points here) and still real, well-formed music data - just not new music. Most likely an editing/assembly artifact: whoever built the sound data left an old copy of the tail of the harmony part lying around when the stream was extended or edited, and it was never deleted." },
        { "id": "cmt_mkrfej", "address": "$64BA", "text": "A FOURTH MUSIC STREAM THAT NOTHING PLAYS. 216 bytes of the same (ticks, note) pairs, $64BA-$6591, terminator pair at $6592.\n\nIt is unreachable, and this is checkable rather than a guess. Only two routines ever seed a voice pointer - StartTune ($5768) and StartMusic ($9170) - and between them they name exactly five stream starts: $5900, $5980, $6000, $6320, $65A0. find_instructions over $13-$16 and $2B-$2C returns 16 and 6 sites and every one of them is inside those two routines or inside the players themselves. Nothing points here.\n\nNor can voice 2 run into it: voice 2 is stopped by the $FF note at $64B2, seven bytes before this starts. (Voice 1 is the one with no $FF test, and it is at $6000, on the other side of the char animation data.)\n\nSo either it is a discarded arrangement left in the binary, or it is reached by something no static walk can see. It reads as a real melody rather than filler, which makes the first more likely." },
        { "id": "cmt_qao211", "address": "$64BA", "text": "Same (tick,note) format as the real music streams: 109 pairs, 406 ticks (~8.1s), ends with a proper $FF terminator at the byte before $64C7... wait see exact address in claim. Sits 6 bytes after the real voice-2 short-tune ($6320-$64B3, ends $FF at $64B2) in the same data area, structurally identical, well-formed melody (notes 0x24-0x3F, a plausible ~2-octave range, durations 1/2/4/6 ticks) -- not noise. VERIFIED unreachable three ways: (1) find_bytes for the literal pointer bytes BA 64 anywhere in the 47K image returns zero hits -- no table, no pushed address, nothing points here; (2) the only two routines anywhere that ever write the voice-pointer zero page cells $13/$14/$15/$16 are sub_5768 and sub_9170 (find_instructions from:$0013 to:$0016), and neither uses this address; (3) find_references shows no inbound JSR/JMP. Nothing static or dynamic reaches this block; it can only be heard by pointing a scenario at it directly." },
        { "id": "cmt_gkt5du", "address": "$65A0", "text": "Third stream of the title-screen 3-part arrangement, same (tick,note) format. Pointer $2B/$2C seeded to $659E by sub_9170 (pre-increments to $65A0 on first read). Played by loc_91BA, chained immediately after the voice1/voice2 player (loc_88F7 ends with JMP loc_91BA). Voice3 also clears the shared $1A active-flag on its own $FF terminator, same as voice2." },
        { "id": "cmt_hffxpv", "address": "$66F8", "text": "Eight more bytes of the leftover assembler source that fills $5870-$58FF, $5F28-$5F7F and $5FA8-$5FFF: \" JUMP\" $00 then a BASIC link $670C. It ends one byte before the zone table starts at $6700, which is presumably why nobody trimmed it." },
        { "id": "cmt_49duka", "address": "$6700", "text": "ZONE 0 of 42: \"ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS··\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $08\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $63\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $00   sprite bank index -> $1000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $00\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_5973la", "address": "$6700", "text": "Zone 0: \"ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS..\"\nHeader at +$98: $08 $00 $63 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_5lh3qt", "address": "$6700", "text": "THE COMPLETE ZONE RECORD, 200 bytes. The first 152 are nineteen tables of eight - one row per attribute, one column per creature type - copied wholesale to $1DA0 by LoadZoneTemplates, so the offset here IS the offset from $1DA0 and every tmpl* label is a slice of this:\n\n  +$00 $1DA0 tmplSpawnInterval    ticks between spawns of this type\n  +$08 $1DA8 tmplMoveType         0 static, 1 linear, 2 accelerating (dispatch at $993A)\n  +$10 $1DB0 tmplAnimFirst        first sprite block of the animation\n  +$18 $1DB8 tmplAnimLast         last one; equal to the first means no animation\n  +$20 $1DC0 tmplAnimMode         low 6 bits are the frame delay, bits 6 and 7 select ping-pong\n  +$28 $1DC8 tmplTypeWhenShot     what it becomes when the bullet hits it\n  +$30 $1DD0 tmplTypeWhenExploded what it becomes when the camel hits it\n  +$38 $1DD8 tmplNextType         what it becomes when its lifetime runs out; $FF means die\n  +$40 $1DE0 tmplSpeedX           sign-magnitude, low 7 bits a countdown\n  +$48 $1DE8 tmplSpeedY           same\n  +$50 $1DF0 tmplField80          step size added to the position each move\n  +$58 $1DF8 tmplSpawnLimit       bit 7 counts against a per-zone cap, bit 6 and the low nibble select the extra behaviour at $9C64\n  +$60 $1E00 tmplSpawnX           spawn column, or 0 to mean random\n  +$68 $1E08 tmplSpawnY           spawn row, or 0 to mean random\n  +$70 $1E10 tmplSpawnMode        0 fixed, $10 random, anything else = place relative to a live object of type (low nibble - 1)\n  +$78 $1E18 tmplColour           sprite colour, bit 7 selects multicolour\n  +$80 $1E20 tmplLifetime         ticks before it turns into tmplNextType\n  +$88 $1E28 tmplScoreValue       low nibble = score digit column, high nibble = how many to add. $15 is 100, $56 is 50, $55 is 500. Zero means shooting it scores nothing and makes no sound\n  +$90 $1E30 tmplExplodeLength    how much neutronium an explosion of this type burns\n\nThen eight scalars read by $9CB5 in this order:\n  +$98 typeMulticolourMask, +$99 typeYExpandMask, +$9A typeXExpandMask,\n  +$9B the object slot limit (8 in 41 zones, 6 in zone 6),\n  +$9C the sprite bank index (only ever 0, 2, 4 or 6),\n  +$9D $D025, +$9E $D026, +$9F read and discarded.\n\nAnd finally +$A0..+$C7, the 40-character zone name in screen codes.\n\nWorked example, zone 0 type 7: interval $A1, move type 2, shape $B8 to $B8, spawn mode 7 - so it is placed on top of a live type 6 - score $55 = 500 points, and it turns into type 0 when shot. Type 0 in the same zone scores nothing, so the wave is a chain: the thing worth 500 becomes something worth nothing." },
        { "id": "cmt_laz2nq", "address": "$6700", "text": "ZONE DATA TABLE. 42 records of 200 ($C8) bytes, $6700-$87CF, immediately below the code at $87FE with 46 bytes of slack.\n\nThe arithmetic that identifies it is at $93D8: $3E/$3F is loaded with $6700 and then $C8 is added once per zone, X coming from zoneNumber ($58). $9699 then copies $98 = 152 bytes from ($3E),Y into $1DA0, so 152 of each 200-byte record are used and 48 are slack.\n\nThe record count is fixed by $9DA0: after a zone, $58 is incremented and compared with #$2A, so zones run 0 to 41 - 42 of them, 42 x 200 = 8400 = $20D0 bytes, $6700 + $20D0 = $87D0. The table ends exactly where the data does.\n\nThe 152 bytes are 19 groups of 8: one row per attribute, one column per object type, since the game has 8 object types per zone. The destination labels give the layout - $1DA0 spawn interval, $1DA8, $1DB0 shape, $1DB8, $1DC0, ... $1DE0 speed X, $1DE8 speed Y, $1DF0, $1DF8 spawn limit, $1E00, $1E10, $1E18 colour, $1E20.\n\nSo a zone is not a map: it is a table of what may spawn, how often, how fast, what shape and what colour. That is the whole level design of this game." },
        { "id": "cmt_m0wzx5", "address": "$6700", "text": "42 zone records of 200 bytes. See the comment at $6700 for the layout and the comment on each record for its name." },
        { "id": "cmt_sd1lpa", "address": "$6700", "text": "THE ATTACK WAVE TABLE. 42 records of 200 ($C8) bytes: $6700 to $87CF. This is the single largest thing in the program and nothing referred to it absolutely, so it was invisible to find_references.\n\nHow the base and stride were established, at $93AB:\n  93D8  LDA #$67 / STA $3F      zoneDataPtr high = $67\n  93DC  LDA #$00 / STA $3E      zoneDataPtr low  = $00\n  93E0  LDX $58 (zoneNumber); loop: zoneDataPtr += $C8, DEX\nSo record n is at $6700 + n*200 and everything in a wave is read through ($3E),Y by sub_9CFB.\n\nWithin a record, bytes $A0-$C7 are the 40-character banner shown when the wave starts. That offset is fixed: every one of the 42 lands exactly on a record boundary + $A0, which is what makes the whole layout provable rather than guessed. Bytes $00-$9F are the wave parameters, distributed by $9CB5 and its callers (sprite multicolours, expand flags, a character set index into charSetSourceTable, and more I did not chase).\n\n42 is confirmed independently by the game's own credits text at $5A00: \"THE GAME CONSISTS OF FORTY TWO DIFFERENT ATTACK WAVES\". The last record $8708-$87CF ends 46 bytes before code resumes at $87FE.\n\nWaves, in order (zoneNumber 0-41, displayed as 1-42):\n 0 ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS!!        21 BUT IT'S ONLY 'SPACE INVADERS'..\n 1 RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST  22 NO CRUISE IS GOOD NEWS!!\n 2 MANIC MINTER                        23 DON'T CALL US, WE'LL CALL EWE..\n 3 WACKY WHACKERS                      24 WHAT IS A BRAHM, ANYWAY?\n 4 ATTACK OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELS  25 ZAPSTARS II: TOTALLY WEIRD..\n 5 THIS KIOSK IS A NUCLEAR FREE ZONE   26 MORE TEA, VICAR?\n 6 HAVEN'T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE?    27 EGGBERT AND THE BOUNCING BOMBS\n 7 AGGRESSIVE AUSTRALIAN ALPINISTS     28 BEWARE OF THE KITCHEN DEVILS!\n 8 ROCK 'N' ROLLUPS                    29 FAST FOOD IS BAD FOR CAMELS\n 9 TELECOM'S COMEUPPANCE               30 SEVERAL SINISTER SPACE SHIPS\n10 HAVE A GO AT THESE...               31 SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS\n11 INKY, PINKY, BLINKY, AND THUD!      32 EGGCENTRICITY II: EWE BETTER DUCK!\n12 FISH 'N' PHOTONS???                 33 THE WACKY WORLD OF WINDSURFING TODAY\n13 THROUGH PASTURES BLUE...            34 BEWARE THE RUBBER KEYS OF DOOM\n14 Z A P S T A R S                     35 IRATAIN ONSLAUGHT\n15 BATTLESCAR GIGANTICA                36 PAC IT IN, ZONE GUNNER!\n16 SILLY SILICON                       37 ATTACK OF THE AWFUL SOFTWARE\n17 JUGGLIN' CHOPTERS                   38 HI FI HASSLES\n18 RADIOACTIVE REMAINS!!               39 ZAPSTARS III: GEOMETRICA\n19 IT'S ME AND EWE, BABE..             40 BEASTLY BONANZA\n20 CAREFUL WITH THAT AXE, EUGENE       41 REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS" },
        { "id": "cmt_xp1569", "address": "$6700", "text": "42 records x 200 bytes = 8400. Record size confirmed from the NEXINI zone-pointer stepping loop at $93E4 (LDA $3E / CLC / ADC #$C8 / STA $3E), which starts from $6700 (LDX #$08/LDA #$67 at $93D3) and never dereferences the table directly -- every access is through ($3E),Y, which is why find_instructions cannot see it. Field offsets for nextType/lifetime/sprite range traced through sub_9772 (spawn) and sub_9A39 (age-out) and confirmed against raw bytes of zone 4 (ATTACK OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELS): type 7 there has spriteFirst=$EC spriteLast=$EE lifetime=12 nextType=$FF, matching a 3-frame explosion animation that ages out after 12 ticks and does not chain into anything else." },
        { "id": "cmt_mqf211", "address": "$67C8", "text": "ZONE 1 of 42: \"RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $04\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $02\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $00   sprite bank index -> $1000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $00\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_r581ge", "address": "$67C8", "text": "Zone 1: \"RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST\"\nHeader at +$98: $04 $00 $02 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_308uwz", "address": "$6890", "text": "Zone 2: \"MANIC MINTER\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_jd99w3", "address": "$6890", "text": "ZONE 2 of 42: \"MANIC  MINTER\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $00   sprite bank index -> $1000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $00\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_bvr3a8", "address": "$6958", "text": "Zone 3: \"WACKY WHACKERS\"\nHeader at +$98: $80 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
