        { "id": "cmt_kl88np", "address": "$8708", "text": "Zone 41: \"REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_4s5kde", "address": "$87D0", "text": "32 zero bytes then 14 x $EA. The wave table ends at $87CF and code begins at $87FE; this is the gap. The $EA run is NOP filler rather than data anyone reads — nothing references it and it decodes to fourteen NOPs falling into the routine at $87FE." },
        { "id": "cmt_6pv9hc", "address": "$87FE", "text": "Already known as an entry point (called from loc_8A3C) but re-checking neighbourhood decode." },
        { "id": "cmt_mceco8", "address": "$87FE", "text": "Builds a 40-entry (X 0..39) table of pointers to each screen row: dat_0340,X = low byte, dat_0360,X = high byte, starting at $0400 and adding $28 (40) each step -- the classic C64 screen-row-address lookup table, presumably to avoid recomputing row*40+col elsewhere." },
        { "id": "cmt_x8bj9j", "address": "$87FE", "text": "Build the screen row address tables at $0340/$0360 that ScreenAddrFromRow reads. $0400 stepping by 40, 27 entries. This is the first thing $8A3C does, before anything else, because nothing can print until it exists." },
        { "id": "cmt_rek7sj", "address": "$8825", "text": "Screen address for printRow, into $48/$49, with Y left holding printColumn so the caller can do STA ($48),Y.\n\nIt does NOT multiply: it reads two tables at $0340 (low) and $0360 (high) in the tape buffer, which is free once the game has taken the machine over. FOUND THE BUILDER: sub_87FE, the very first thing the game does at $8A3C. It walks $0400 upward in steps of 40 for $1B = 27 entries.\n\nTwenty-seven, not twenty-five. Rows 25 and 26 come out as $07E8 and $0810 - past the end of the screen, into the sprite pointers at $07F8 and then into sprite data. Nothing appears to print there, and PrintCharAt only guards the COLUMN, not the row, so a routine that got its row wrong would corrupt the sprite pointers rather than fail visibly. Worth knowing if anything ever looks wrong there.\n\n$8834 is five orphaned bytes, 20 25 88 B1 48 = JSR $8825 / LDA ($48),Y, followed by the RTS at $8839 that PrintCharAt branches to. That is a read-character-at routine whose own entry point was deleted, leaving its body reachable only as PrintCharAt off-screen exit. Harmless, and a clear sign of hand editing." },
        { "id": "cmt_gpkmq2", "address": "$8834", "text": "JSR ScreenAddrFromRow / LDA ($48),Y - the body of a read-character-at routine whose entry point is gone. The RTS after it at $8839 is live: PrintCharAt branches there when the column is off screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_ox6815", "address": "$8834", "text": "Forcing decode of a small undecoded gap between two already-decoded routines; bytes looked like plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_fhpf95", "address": "$883A", "text": "Writes A to screen via ($48),Y, then adds #$D4 to the pointer's high byte ($49) -- turning a screen-RAM pointer ($04xx-$07xx) into the matching Color-RAM pointer ($D8xx-$DBxx, since colour RAM = screen + $D400 -- $04+$D4=$D8) -- and writes a second byte (from $05) as the colour at the same offset. X (compared to #$28=40) looks like a column bound check. Called from many places (16 refs); the closest thing this program has to a print-character-with-colour primitive." },
        { "id": "cmt_mfwim5", "address": "$883A", "text": "PRINT ONE CHARACTER. printColumn $02, printRow $03, printChar $04, printColour $05. Preserves X and Y.\n\nRefuses silently if the column is $28 or more - BPL on CMP #$28, so it also refuses columns $80-$FF, which is what makes the star code safe when a star wraps past column 0 to $FF.\n\nThe colour address is the screen address plus $D400, which is $0400 + $D400 = $D800. So this one instruction is the proof that the screen is at $0400 and the colour map at $D800.\n\nbasalt asked for this name in chat; it was in my block, so I have put it in." },
        { "id": "cmt_whjnwo", "address": "$886F", "text": "Take the machine over: raster IRQ in, NMI disabled, VIC into multicolour text.\n- CINV = $88D5, the raster handler.\n- NMINV = $88D4, which is a bare RTI. That is the standard way to make RESTORE do nothing.\n- $0291 = $80 disables the Commodore+SHIFT charset toggle.\n- $D01A = $81 enables the raster interrupt source; $D012 = $60 and $D011 bit 7 cleared put the split at raster line $60 (96).\n- $D016: AND #$F7 clears CSEL (38-column, so the side borders open for smooth scrolling) and ORA #$10 sets MCM (multicolour text).\n- $D418 = $0F, full volume.\nAlso seeds the music tempo: $10 = $11 = 7." },
        { "id": "cmt_bktl77", "address": "$88D4", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_h9zb8e", "address": "$88D4", "text": "Correction: this single RTI byte is separate from the real handler, which starts at $88D5 (raster_irq_handler_body) -- the disassembler stops at RTI so it does not fall through. edit_claim cannot revise a claim's comment or method, only name/is/extent/root, so this follow-up comment stands next to the original rather than replacing it." },
        { "id": "cmt_nzxjam", "address": "$88D4", "text": "Raster IRQ handler (installed dynamically -- the reset path at $C0B9 sets the IRQ vector to the stock $EA31, so something later repoints it here). RTI as first byte is a stray leftover from an earlier RTI-terminated stub; the real body follows: LDA VICIRQ; AND #$01; BNE stock-IRQ($EA31); INC $1E (frame counter); JMP $8BC9 (main per-frame update, already claimed as a routine)." },
        { "id": "cmt_onehy8", "address": "$88D4", "text": "One byte, $40 = RTI, installed as the NMI vector at $0318. Pressing RESTORE therefore does nothing. It is also the byte immediately before the IRQ handler, so the two vectors are $88D4 and $88D5 - deliberate, not a coincidence." },
        { "id": "cmt_upfe5c", "address": "$88D4", "text": "Installed as NMINV ($0318/$0319) by sub_886F. A single RTI - NMI is effectively disabled/ignored." },
        { "id": "cmt_4r48to", "address": "$88D5", "text": "Raster IRQ handler. If $D019 bit 0 is clear this was not the raster, so hand it to the KERNAL at $EA31 (the CIA timer IRQ is still running and still needs servicing). Otherwise bump the frame counter and go to the raster-split code at $8BC9.\nEverything below - $88E4/$88EC/$88F7/$91BA - is the tail of that handler: it finishes by acknowledging $D019 and falling into $EA31 to restore registers and RTI." },
        { "id": "cmt_dbcgel", "address": "$88D5", "text": "Installed as CINV ($0314/$0315) by sub_886F (STA CINV #$88D5) during init. Reached only via the CPU IRQ vector, never by JSR/JMP, so the static walk never finds it without this claim. Handles the raster split, the SID music player, and (via $8BC9) the sprite/cheat-key logic." },
        { "id": "cmt_km1k99", "address": "$88D5", "text": "Separate from $88D4 (a bare RTI, one byte -- a stub/placeholder IRQ used before install, or the tail of alignment; the disassembler treats RTI as ending flow so this had to be rooted on its own). LDA VICIRQ; AND #$01; BNE stock-IRQ($EA31); INC $1E (frame counter); JMP $8BC9 (main per-frame update)." },
        { "id": "cmt_6o813b", "address": "$88F7", "text": "Music player, voices 1 and 2, run once per raster interrupt. This and $91BA are three copies of one routine.\nA tune is a stream of (ticks, note) byte pairs. The pointer is advanced by 2 BEFORE the read, which is why StartMusic seeds it two below the data: $13/$14 = $5FFE so the first pair read is at $6000.\nPer voice: decrement the tick counter; when it hits zero, gate the voice off (STY $D404 with Y=0), step the pointer, read ticks into $17/$19/$2D and the note into A.\n  note $F0 - a rest. Leave the voice gated off for this many ticks.\n  note $FF - end of tune. Clears $1A so nothing plays until StartMusic is called again. (Voice 1 has no $FF test, only voices 2 and 3 do, so voice 1 relies on the others to stop the tune.)\n  anything else - index into noteFreqLo/noteFreqHi at $898C/$89CD (65 notes) and gate on with $21 = triangle + gate.\n$5F non-zero suppresses the gate-on in all three voices while leaving the frequency writes, which is how the tune is silenced without losing its place.\n$10/$11 is a tempo divider: the whole player only runs every $11 interrupts." },
        { "id": "cmt_fx2l04", "address": "$88FE", "text": "Five orphaned bytes, A9 60 8D 12 D0 = LDA #$60 / STA $D012. Nothing branches here: the JMP at $88FB goes over it and the music player continues at $8903. Setting the raster back to $60 is what the phase dispatcher at $8BC9 now does, so this is the old version left in place when that moved. Declared code so it reads as what it is rather than as five stray bytes." },
        { "id": "cmt_l92grt", "address": "$88FE", "text": "Forcing decode of a small undecoded gap between two already-decoded routines; bytes looked like plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_5qotng", "address": "$898C", "text": "Note frequency tables, 65 entries each: low bytes at $898C-$89CC, high bytes at $89CD-$8A0D. Indexed by the note byte in a tune, so a note is 0..64. The high bytes run 02..54, which is the usual five-and-a-bit octave equal-tempered table for a PAL SID." },
        { "id": "cmt_e50uck", "address": "$898C", "text": "Low byte of a SID voice-1 frequency value, indexed by X = a note number read from a note/duration byte stream via LDA ($13),Y at $88F7 (loc_88F7 is a music sequencer: reads note at Y=0, duration/flag at Y=1 checked against terminator $F0, advances the 16-bit pointer $13/14 by 2 per step). Paired with sid_note_freq_hi at $89CD -- two parallel arrays (lo table, hi table) rather than one interleaved record, so add_type (which declares fields within one element) does not fit this shape; recording the pairing in these two comments/names is the workaround." },
        { "id": "cmt_m9hq5z", "address": "$898C", "text": "SID V1FREQLO table, indexed by note code 0-64. Paired with NoteFreqHi at $89CD (same index). Computed each entry as a SID frequency word and converted to Hz at PAL clock (985248.4 Hz, hz = reg*clock/2^24): index 0 = 31.48 Hz, and every +12 index doubles the frequency exactly (index 12=62.95, 24=125.97, 36=251.99, 48=504.04) -- true equal temperament, tuned about a quarter-tone flat of concert pitch (A440 would put A at index ~45=44.9 vs the actual table)." },
        { "id": "cmt_4remu0", "address": "$89CD", "text": "High byte of the SID voice-1 frequency value for the same note index X as sid_note_freq_lo at $898C (STA V1FREQLO / STA V1FREQHI at $88F7). Values rise roughly exponentially ($02..$54), consistent with a chromatic-note frequency table." },
        { "id": "cmt_24bt9t", "address": "$8A0E", "text": "Three 12-byte tables read 1-based: column at $8A0E, row at $8A1A, colour at $8A26, then titleFlashColours at $8A32." },
        { "id": "cmt_ws25dz", "address": "$8A0E", "text": "Tail of the $898C-$8A3B span, right before loc_8A3C. No absolute-address reference found into this span. Bytes: 03 13 21 16 0F 09 25 1B 07 12 20 19 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 02 0B 06 07 08 03 04 06 07 09 03 08 05 0A 07 09 03 01 07 03 05 04 06 00 54 57 4F. Small values throughout, several short ascending runs (03 04 05 06 07 08 09 appears twice) -- guessing a further small table (durations? note-stream data reached only through the $13/14 pointer this project has not traced to an initial value?) but not confirmed. Last 3 bytes read as ASCII \"TWO\" -- possibly incidental, possibly the tail of a nearby string this claim's extent cut into; worth re-checking if $8A3C's neighbourhood changes." },
        { "id": "cmt_5k1ox6", "address": "$8A39", "text": "The three letters T W O. sub_8A42 copies them over offsets 9-11 of the player two status line, turning the copied template PLAYER ONE into PLAYER TWO. Note the loop runs X = 3,2,1 and stops before X = 0, so $8A38 is never read - the string is $8A39, not $8A38." },
        { "id": "cmt_cprv6b", "address": "$8A62", "text": "NEW GAME INIT - and this is where every starting value in the game is set. Reached from $8A3C after the screen row table is built.\n\nClears about twenty zero page bytes to zero, blanks the border and background, then:\n  $1F = 4     the fine scroll offset\n  $4A = $12   EIGHTEEN units of neutronium - the full bar\n  $4B = 3     drain sub-tick\n  $4C = 6     top of the neutronium colour ramp\n  $5A = 3     attract scroll state\n  $3F = $67   high byte of the zone data pointer, so it starts at $6700 = zone 0\n  $57 = 7     zone distance, the default the options screen edits\n  $54 = 5     FIVE BEASTS - the starting lives\n  $40 = 8     camel colour\n  $5D = $5A, $59 = 0, $5C = 0\n\nThen InstallIrqAndVicMode takes the machine over, the bottom five rows ($0720-$07E7) are filled with character $28 in colour 6 - the ground - and the VIC gets multicolour text, background colours and $D018 = $18 for screen $0400 / characters $2000.\n\nSo: 5 beasts, 18 neutronium, zone 0, distance 7. Everything else the player sees is derived from the zone record." },
        { "id": "cmt_yodwsl", "address": "$8AEA", "text": "Seed the 12 stars, then take a snapshot of the player state.\n\nThe seeds come from three 12-byte tables that are indexed 1-BASED - LDA table-1,X with X = $0C down to 1 - which is why the operands read as $8A0D, $8A19 and $8A25 rather than as the tables themselves. $8A0D really is the last entry of noteFreqHi, so the listing showing noteFreqHi + $0040,X is correct and not a mis-resolution.\n  starInitColumn $8A0E   03 13 21 16 0F 09 25 1B 07 12 20 19   all below $28, so screen columns\n  starInitRow    $8A1A   03 04 05 06 07 08 09 02 0B 06 07 08   all below $19, so screen rows\n  starColour     $8A26   03 04 06 07 09 03 08 05 0A 07 09 03   all below $10, so colours\nThat the three tables fall in those three ranges and nowhere else is what identifies which is which; nothing in the code says so.\nEvery star starts at glyph $1F and colour cycle 1.\n\n$8B05 then copies zoneNumber, loopNumber and beastsRemaining into BOTH players saved-state blocks at $5F30 and $5FB0, which is how a new game gives player two the same starting position as player one." },
        { "id": "cmt_bpqbkq", "address": "$8B5F", "text": "Write one object position into the sprite shadow registers, and maintain the X most-significant-bit mask.\n\nX is the object slot. Sprite index is (X AND 7) so slots 0-7 and 8-15 share the eight hardware sprites - this game multiplexes two sets of eight over the raster split. $07 is the X coordinate in character-ish units and is doubled, $08 the Y. They go to sprX $1F40 and sprY $1F50.\n\nThe carry out of ASL is the ninth X bit, and it is folded into sprMsbXMaskFrame0 $1F9C or $1F9D depending on bit 3 of the slot number - one mask per half of the multiplex. Clearing goes through EOR #$FF / AND and setting through ORA, which is why the two halves of this routine look duplicated: they are the same shift with opposite bit operations." },
        { "id": "cmt_j7trvv", "address": "$8B5F", "text": "No absolute-address inbound reference found; forcing decode as a routine since the bytes disassemble cleanly as 6502 and dat_A000 (a jump table at $A000, see sub_8D1D) plausibly reaches it indirectly." },
        { "id": "cmt_sjp46b", "address": "$8B5F", "text": "X (masked to 0-7, then +1) selects a sprite. Stores zp $08 to dat_1F50+X and zp $07*2 to dat_1F40+X (shadow copies, not the real $D000 sprite registers -- probably built once per frame and blitted to hardware elsewhere so sprites do not tear mid-raster). Carry out of the ASL (i.e. bit 7 of the un-doubled value) sets or clears one bit of a 2-byte table at dat_1F9C, built via a shift-loop rather than a lookup -- the classic idiom for a sprite MSB/multicolour/priority register shadow, but I have not traced dat_1F9C to $D010 to confirm which VIC register it shadows." },
        { "id": "cmt_gsyju9", "address": "$8BAF", "text": "Immediately follows the RTS ending set_sprite_shadow_xy_and_msb; disassembles cleanly as 6502. Forcing decode -- likely reached the same way (indirectly / by a caller elsewhere not yet traced)." },
        { "id": "cmt_c870h2", "address": "$8BC9", "text": "RASTER PHASE DISPATCH. $1E is not really a frame counter, it is a phase counter 0-1-2, bumped by the IRQ at $88DF, and this picks which of three raster splits has just fired:\n\n  phase 1  -> next raster $D8, and $D016 X-scroll bits set from scrollX ($1F)\n  phase 2  -> next raster $F8, X-scroll bits cleared and $D011 bit 7 cleared\n  phase 0  -> next raster $90; this is the one that does the work\n\nSo the play field scrolls smoothly between rasters $D8 and $F8 and the panel above it does not, which is the classic split-scroll.\n\nThe work phase reads $D01E (sprite/sprite collision) into $44 - reading it clears it, so this is the only place collisions are latched - then runs the key check, the title flash, object movement every other frame ($20), and the horizontal scroll.\n\nSCROLLING: DEC $1F each pass. At 4 it calls AnimateCharacters. When it passes $FF it reloads 7 and shifts whole character columns left by one, the loop at $8C53 copying $0630 to $062F and so on. Fine scroll for seven steps, coarse scroll on the eighth - textbook, and it means the play field is character-mapped, not bitmapped." },
        { "id": "cmt_gn0sbi", "address": "$8BC9", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_v36n4z", "address": "$8BC9", "text": "Entered from the raster IRQ (raster_irq_handler_body, $88D5) on every raster interrupt. Dispatches on zp $1E (a phase counter, reset to 0 in the phase-3 arm below, otherwise not seen incremented in this routine -- the IRQ handler only does INC $1E before jumping here, so $1E cycles 0,1,2,0,1,2..): phase 1 sets the next raster split at $D8 and re-derives SCROLX from $1F; phase 2 sets the split at $F8, turns on the top of the display (clears SCROLY bit7) and rounds SCROLX down; the fallthrough (phase 0 / anything else) is the heavy one -- reads sprite-sprite collision (SPSPCL->$44), arms the next split at $90, calls sub_96D2 (a special-key check gate) and conditionally sub_508B, then runs a second, separate counter at $1F (0..7) that round-robins per-frame housekeeping: score/HUD countdowns at $2A/$29, a wrapping scroll counter at absolute dat_1F70 (not the per-object array of the same address -- a real address collision, not an alias), the wave animation trigger (JMP loc_9206) on count 4, and a table walk over dat_0630 on the rest. I decoded and covered all of this (no undecoded bytes remain in it) but did not chase every branch target to a name -- covered, not fully understood in the sense the brief means for the class of \"say what things are made of\"." },
        { "id": "cmt_71c0xb", "address": "$8CA3", "text": "Forcing decode of a small undecoded gap between two already-decoded routines; bytes looked like plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_lj7fqx", "address": "$8CA3", "text": "4 dead bytes: the JMP at $8CA0 lands at $8CA7, skipping these entirely. Forcing them to decode as code (BIT/EOR) produced an overlap warning against the real code at $8CA7 -- reverted. Bytes: 2C 3B 41 45." },
        { "id": "cmt_lkwztd", "address": "$8D1D", "text": "THE RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR, and it does not read RAM.\n\n  INC $24 / LDX $24 / LDA $A000,X / RTS\n\n$A000-$BFFF in this image is 8192 bytes of ZERO — I checked every page. If this read RAM the generator would return 0 forever and eleven callers would all take the same branch every time.\n\nIt does not read RAM. Nothing in this program writes $00 or $01 (I searched: zero instructions touch the processor port), so the 6510 port keeps its power-on $37 and BASIC ROM is banked in over $A000-$BFFF. This reads a byte of BASIC ROM and calls it a random number.\n\nThat is a well-worn C64 trick and it is why the game needs no seed and no arithmetic: the ROM is a fixed 8K block of high-entropy-looking bytes that is always there. It is also perfectly deterministic — the same $24 gives the same byte on every machine and every run — which is the sort of thing that matters if anyone ever tries to reproduce a wave.\n\nEleven callers: $8CD4, $8CE1, $8D6F, $8DAC, $8EB9, $8F31, $8F3A, $9387, $97EF, $9804, $9858. TitleColourAndSoundStep at $50BD reads the same table directly, taking AND #$03 of it to pick a border colour.\n\nre64 cannot see any of this: the memory model is flat and a project layer supplying $A000 wins over a ROM nobody has loaded. The zeros are real bytes of the runtime image; they are just never what the CPU reads." },
        { "id": "cmt_rcm069", "address": "$8D1D", "text": "INC $24; LDX $24; LDA $A000,X; RTS -- a free-running index into BASIC ROM used as the entropy source, 11 callers. Confirmed with the machine target (BASIC ROM now linked at $A000): the bytes there are real ROM content (CBMBASIC0 signature visible right at $A000), not zeros. On the runtime target alone (no ROM loaded) this reads as 8192 zero bytes and the RNG looks broken; it is deterministic on real hardware -- same counter position, same byte, every run." },
        { "id": "cmt_0u7fwr", "address": "$8D25", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_9d5bum", "address": "$8D2F", "text": "THE TERRAIN, and this is what the game is scrolling.\n\nThis writes six characters into $0657, $067F, $06A7, $06CF, $06F7, $071F. Those are 40 apart, so they are ONE VERTICAL COLUMN of the screen: $0657-$0400 = 599 = row 14, column 39. The rightmost column, rows 14 to 19. The colour byte goes to the matching $DA57.. cells, which is $D800+599, confirming colour RAM under a $0400 screen.\n\nSo new ground arrives at the right-hand edge one column at a time, which is how a character-scrolled landscape is fed. loc_8A62 primes the rest of it at $8AC6: char #$28 and colour #$06 into $071F,X and $DB1F,X for X = $C8 down to 1, which is rows 20-24 filled solid blue.\n\nThe column is staged in newColumnChars $1FF1-$1FF6 before it is written, so the profile can be built up over several frames." },
        { "id": "cmt_i5562p", "address": "$8DF4", "text": "TERRAIN PROFILE TABLES. Two of them, six bytes per frame, one frame per screen column.\n\n  terrainProfileSmall $8DF4  6 frames  = a low hill\n  terrainProfileLarge $8E24  10 frames = a tall one\n\nRead as six-byte rows they draw a hillside, top of the column first:\n\n  20 20 20 20 20 26      . . . . . /\n  20 20 20 20 26 28      . . . . / #\n  20 20 20 26 28 28      . . . / # #\n  20 20 20 27 28 28      . . . \\ # #\n  20 20 20 20 27 28      . . . . \\ #\n  20 20 20 20 20 27      . . . . . \\\n\n$20 is blank, $28 is solid ground, and $26 and $27 are the rising and falling slope glyphs of the game font. Loop over the frames and you get a hill that climbs and comes back down.\n\nloc_8E74 chooses between them on $22 (CMP #$03 takes the large one) and indexes with $23 * 8, then copies six bytes into newColumnChars. $8EB9 seeds $22 from NextRandom AND #$07 ORA #$01, so the landscape is generated from BASIC ROM bytes.\n\nThe last two bytes of each 8-byte row are $00 $00 and are not copied — the loop runs Y = 1..6." },
        { "id": "cmt_pgo0cd", "address": "$8DF4", "text": "16 frames of 8 screen-codes each (indexed as frame*8+column by loc_8E74/loc_8E9A: X = frame_index*8, then 6 of the 8 bytes per frame are copied to dat_1FF0,Y, one column at a time). Screen codes $20 (blank) and $26-$2A (5 distinct glyphs) trace out a peak sliding left-right across the 8 columns and rising/falling through the 5 glyphs -- a wave/pulse animation, most likely the moving indicator I saw named NEUTRONIUM STATUS in hud_status_text ($90E4). Frame selection depends on zero-page $22/$23 (compared against #$03), not yet traced further." },
        { "id": "cmt_qpdkk3", "address": "$8DF4", "text": "Two hill profiles for the scrolling landscape: six frames at $8DF4, ten at $8E24, six bytes of screen codes each plus two unused. See the comment at $8DF4." },
        { "id": "cmt_5njxb4", "address": "$8F7C", "text": "THE TITLE SCREEN LOOP. Starfield, INC $D025 so the sprite multicolour cycles, StartMusic, the options key check, and then read the joystick fire button - anything but fire loops back here forever.\n\nStartMusic returns immediately if a tune is already playing, so calling it every pass is how the title music restarts when it ends rather than being started once.\n\nPress fire and $8F92 takes over: silence the tune, build both player status lines, copy the chosen zone distance into the player two line, and start the game at $937B." },
        { "id": "cmt_a7jg34", "address": "$8FCB", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_4a4wcu", "address": "$8FD2", "text": "THE STARFIELD - STARF in the leftover source at $5FA8, where the main loop reads MLOOP JSR STARF / JSR BULITZ / JSR CCAMEL. All three are now identified: STARF = $8FB0 driving this, BULITZ = $95B9, CCAMEL = $9444.\n\nOne star per call, index in X, 12 stars (1..12). Four parallel arrays: starColumn $1F00, starRow $1F10, starGlyph $1F20, starDelay $1F30.\n\nThe glyph counts DOWN from $1F to $1B, so a star is drawn as one of five characters and dims as it ages. When it reaches $1B the star is blanked with a space, the glyph resets to $1F and the column decreases by one - so a star moves one column left every five updates and twinkles in between. At column $FF it wraps to $27 (column 39, the right edge).\n\nCORRECTION to what I first wrote here: $8A26,X is not a colour, it is the per-star DELAY reload, written back into $1F30,X on every update. $8FB0 decrements $1F30,X each tick and only calls this when it reaches zero, so each star has its own period - 03 04 06 07 09 03 08 05 0A 07 09 03 ticks. That is PARALLAX: twelve stars at nine different speeds. The colour is not per star at all; $8FB5 sets printColour to 1 for the whole field." },
        { "id": "cmt_jui7ga", "address": "$9091", "text": "Copies dat_90F7 (\"NEUTRONIUM STATUS  HHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH\") into screen+colour RAM. Every byte is AND #$3F (ASCII into the low 64-code range the custom font shares with the message line) EXCEPT when the source byte is literally ASCII H (#$48, CMP #$48/BEQ skips the mask) - that one byte is written unmasked. Glyph $48 in the character set at $2000 (offset $2240) is a solid horizontal bar (00 00 FF FF FF FF FF 00), not a letter - the masked H glyph at code $08 ($2040: 66 66 66 7E E6 E6 E6 00) is a normal H. So the neutronium gauge is a string of literal H characters in the source text that render as a solid bar because this one routine deliberately lets that one byte through unmasked, reaching a second character bank the rest of the game never touches." },
        { "id": "cmt_8273z3", "address": "$90E4", "text": "\"OF THE MUTANT CAMELS\", 20 characters. Read by sub_9019 at $907E. The rest of the title is elsewhere - this is only the second line." },
        { "id": "cmt_hzy7rw", "address": "$90E4", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_th9cj8", "address": "$90E4", "text": "Read via read_bytes, not disassembly -- an earlier attempt to force-decode this span as code produced garbage (SRE/illegal opcodes, JSRs to nonsense addresses). Full text: \"OF THE MUTANT CAMELS  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM    NEUTRONIUM STATUS  HHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH  \". The leading \"OF THE MUTANT CAMELS\" is the tail of the game title (REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS); the rest are score/status HUD field labels." },
        { "id": "cmt_hrmixc", "address": "$90F8", "text": "The 40-column status line, copied verbatim into both players lines at $5F00 and $5F80 by sub_8A42, which ANDs each byte with #$3F to turn PETSCII upper case into screen codes.\n  \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \"\nThat fixes the field offsets used everywhere else: score is the seven digits at offset 14, the zone distance is the two digits at offset 33, and the word ONE is at offsets 9-11. sub_8A42 overwrites those three bytes of the SECOND copy with \"TWO\" from $8A39, which is the whole of what makes $5F80 the player two line." },
        { "id": "cmt_v73ci1", "address": "$9120", "text": "The other 40-column panel line: \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  HHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH \". The 18 H characters are the neutronium bar, and $915E holds exactly 18 colour bytes for them (07 07 07 04 04 04 repeated - yellow/purple in threes), which is what identifies the bar as 18 segments rather than a word." },
        { "id": "cmt_pbq0d6", "address": "$9149", "text": "Follows hud_status_text. 21 bytes of $01 then 07 07 07 04 04 04 repeated three times. Guess: a full/default neutronium status bar -- $01 as a bar-segment glyph, the 6-byte repeat as a colour or animation cycle for it -- not confirmed against the code that copies it to screen/colour RAM." },
        { "id": "cmt_afanhh", "address": "$9170", "text": "Start the tune. Returns immediately if $1A says one is already playing.\nSets the three voice pointers two bytes below their data, because the player adds 2 before reading:\n  voice 1  $13/$14 = $5FFE  ->  tune at $6000\n  voice 2  $15/$16 = $631E  ->  tune at $6320\n  voice 3  $2B/$2C = $659E  ->  tune at $65A0\nThe round destinations are what make this readable; the seeds themselves are not addresses of anything.\nADSR: attack/decay $0A on voices 1 and 2, $0C on voice 3, sustain/release 0 on all three - a plucked note with no sustain. Filter off, volume $0F. All three tick counters start at 1 so the first note is fetched on the very next interrupt." },
        { "id": "cmt_0n9uuq", "address": "$91BA", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_wkap8w", "address": "$91BA", "text": "Third-voice (bass) player, same tick/note stepping logic as loc_88F7 (voice1/voice2), reading via pointer $2B/$2C, writing V3FREQLO/V3FREQHI ($D40E/$D40F). Chained from loc_88F7 unconditionally every raster IRQ. Shares the $1A active-flag with voice2: whichever of voice2/voice3 hits its own $FF terminator first clears $1A for all three." },
        { "id": "cmt_37w9aj", "address": "$9206", "text": "basalt, from outside this block. Character animation, driven off frame counter $2F cycling 0-5 through charAnimFrameOrder ($9259) = 00 01 02 03 02 01, a ping-pong.\n\nFrame f gives offsets $0E = f*$20 and $0F = f*$10, then:\n  32 bytes from charAnimFrames32 ($6240 + $0E) -> $2110   = characters $22-$25\n  16 bytes from charAnimFrames16 ($62C0 + $0F) -> $2148   = characters $29-$2A\n\n$2110 and $2148 place the character set at $2000, which agrees with the only write to VMCSB in the program: $8AE7 stores #$18, so screen $0400 and character base $2000. Four frames of each, so $6240-$62BF and $62C0-$62FF." },
        { "id": "cmt_munaxw", "address": "$9259", "text": "6-entry symmetric ramp (0,1,2,3,2,1) read by LDA dat_9259,X in loc_9206 and stored to dat_2148,Y in a 16-iteration loop -- a triangle-wave index table, the same idiom as wave_anim_frames at $8DF4 but shorter and numeric rather than glyphs." },
        { "id": "cmt_l5ujlz", "address": "$925F", "text": "COMMIT THE SPRITES. There is a two-bank frame multiplexer here and IT NEVER RUNS IN THIS BUILD. Both halves matter, so take them apart carefully.\n\nThe machinery: $30 toggles 0/1 every call. $D015 gets sprEnableMaskFrame0 masked by $32 ORed with the mask for the current phase, and $D010 the same for the X most-significant bits. Then for X = 8 down to 1 it looks at sprInUse[$1F87+X] - that is slots 8 to 15 - and draws hardware sprite X-1 from bank 0 via $929D or bank 1 via $92CF depending on whether that slot is occupied and which phase it is. Sixteen object slots, eight sprites, alternating frames.\n\nWhy it is inert: nothing ever occupies slots 8-15. SSET marks sprInUse[Y] and every caller passes Y in 0..7 - FindFreeObjectSlot only searches 2 to $42-1 and $42 is 8 in 41 of the 42 zones and 6 in the other. ClearObjectSlots at $8B20 writes $FF into sprInUse[0] and sprInUse[8] and zero everywhere else, and nothing clears or sets $1F89-$1F8F afterwards. So the test at $9283 finds zero for X = 2..8 and takes $929D every time, and $1F88 is $FF which $92CF refuses outright. Bank 1 - the shadow registers at $1F48/$1F58/$1F68/$1F78 - is written by nothing and read by nothing.\n\nSo the game shows eight sprites, always from slots 0-7, with no flicker. The multiplexer is either unfinished or was disabled by narrowing $42 to 8 in every zone. If a zone record ever carried $42 greater than 8 the second bank would light up, which makes that byte the switch.\n\n(This corrects what I first wrote here, which said objects 8-15 flicker at 25Hz. The code says they would; the data says there are none.)\n\n$92CB and $92F2 both write $07F8-$07FF, the sprite shape pointers, which independently confirms the screen is at $0400." },
        { "id": "cmt_yr3yb5", "address": "$925F", "text": "INC $30/AND #$01 toggles the multiplex parity flag each frame. Loop X=8..1 over the 8 hardware sprites; for each, calls sub_929D (parity even) or sub_92CF (parity odd), which read from two disjoint 8-slot field arrays ($1F40-7F split in half) for X/Y/colour/pointer and write SP0X/SP0Y/SPMC1/the real VIC sprite-pointer table at dat_1F87... wait: at screen+$3F8 (dat_07F7,X). Two groups of 8 live-object slots (16 total) time-share 8 hardware sprites, one group per frame -- this is the two shadow banks the prior-run memo describes, more precisely." },
        { "id": "cmt_smhk6p", "address": "$92F6", "text": "SSET - and that is the author own name, matched instruction for instruction against the leftover assembler source at $5870.\n\nThe source fragment reads:\n  13880   STA $07F7,X\n  13890   RTS\n  13900  SSET TYA\n  13910   PHA\n  13920   AND #$07\n  13930   SEC\n  13940   TAY\n  13950   INY\n  13960   LDA #$00\n  13970  TANGE ROL A\n  13980   DEY\n  13990   BNE TANGE\n\nAnd the code here is $92F2 STA $07F7,X / $92F5 RTS / $92F6 TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 / $92FF ROL A / DEY / BNE $92FF. Every instruction, in order, including the RTS and the STA of the routine before it. So $92CF is the routine those first two lines end, $92F6 is SSET and $92FF is TANGE.\n\nThat is the only place in this project where a name is taken from the source rather than inferred, and it is safe because the match is exact rather than thematic.\n\nWhat it does: allocate a sprite for object slot Y. Build 1<<(Y AND 7), OR it into sprEnableMaskFrame0 or Frame1 depending on bit 3 of the slot - so slots 0-7 and 8-15 go to different halves of the multiplex - and mark sprInUse[Y] = $88. Slots 8-15 additionally flip their bit in $32, which CommitSprites uses to decide which half is showing this frame." },
        { "id": "cmt_rurndp", "address": "$9328", "text": "Free the sprite for object slot Y: sprInUse[Y] = 0 and the bit cleared from whichever enable mask SSET put it in. The same shift loop as SSET, so this is SSET written out again with AND/EOR instead of ORA. Nine callers - anything that kills an object comes through here." },
        { "id": "cmt_dmda3q", "address": "$93F7", "text": "THE MAIN LOOP, and the leftover assembler source at $5FA8 names three of its four calls:\n\n  15240  MLOOP JSR STARF     ->  $8FB0 UpdateStarfield\n  15250        JSR BULITZ    ->  $95B9 FireAndMoveBullet\n  15260        JSR CCAMEL    ->  $9444 ControlCamel\n\nThe fourth call here, $96FC, is the spawner and creature update; either it came after CCAMEL in the source and the fragment stops before it, or it was added later. Nothing else in this program is a JMP-to-self loop of JSRs, and the three names line up with what the three routines do, so the identification is as firm as it can be without more source.\n\nIt never exits. Everything that ends a life or a zone leaves through a JMP - ZoneComplete at $9D59 even resets the stack pointer first." },
        { "id": "cmt_gcut3b", "address": "$9406", "text": "Clear the 40-character message line ($058F-$05B6) to spaces, then TAIL-JUMP into ShowCheatModeBanner ($57D1) rather than return - so clearing the line immediately puts \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" back if cheatFlag is set. That is why ShowCheatModeBanner ends by setting messageTimer ($4F) = 7: the banner is a message like any other and re-arms its own countdown.\n\nEight callers, all of them \"I have finished with the message line\"." },
        { "id": "cmt_gw1wor", "address": "$9406", "text": "Blank the message line: 40 spaces over $0590-$05B7, then fall into ShowCheatModeBanner, which redraws the cheat banner if the cheat is on. Eight callers - anything that finishes with the message line comes through here, and the fall-through rather than a JSR is why the banner survives every clear." },
        { "id": "cmt_kkuupt", "address": "$9413", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_yri4xn", "address": "$9413", "text": "Drag every object with bit 7 of objMoveType set one column to the left, stopping at X = $10. That bit is the with-the-scroll flag: objects that belong to the terrain move with it, objects that fly do not. Called once every other frame from the raster work phase at $8C20, which is the same cadence the terrain scrolls at." },
        { "id": "cmt_3td8qy", "address": "$942F", "text": "Push all sixteen object positions into the sprite shadow registers through WriteSpritePosition. Called first thing in CommitSprites, so the shadow registers are always one full pass behind the object updates - which is what stops a sprite being torn between its old X and its new Y." },
        { "id": "cmt_dnq2wa", "address": "$9444", "text": "beryl (this is inside amber·s block — comment only, I have named nothing here):\n\nTHIS IS THE PLAYER. Joystick port 2 at $DC00, active low: bit 0 up, bit 1 down, bit 2 left, bit 3 right, bit 4 fire.\n\n  right ($9472 AND #$08 clear) -> INC objXPos (slot 0), stop at $90\n  left  ($948E AND #$04 clear) -> stop at $11, set objFieldFB0 = $80 and sprShape = #$C0\n  fire+up   ($94C0) -> $39 = $0F, $3A = the right bit, $33 = 1, sprShape = #$CD\n  fire+down ($94D7) -> $33 = 2, sprShape = #$CE, objFieldFB0 = $80\n\nSo objFieldFB0 (and $29 beside it) is which way the player faces, $33 is the action state — 0 on foot, 1 and 2 the two fire moves — $39 counts a jump down and $3A remembers whether it was launched moving right.\n\nsprShape with no index is $1F70, virtual sprite slot 0. That pins slot 0 as the player and closes the loop on the object arrays: sub_95B9 fires by putting a shot at objXPos+$13, objYPos+2 into SLOT 1.\n\nThe shape numbers confirm the memory map. #$C0, #$CD and #$CE are sprite blocks, so their data is at $C0*64 = $3000, $3340 and $3380 — all inside the residentSprites region at $2800-$3FFF, which is addressable by the VIC directly and is why those sprites are never copied anywhere. The shot·s shape #$DA at $93D5 is $3680, same region.\n\n$34 divides the frame rate by $80 for this routine; $46 non-zero jumps to ZoneComplete." },
        { "id": "cmt_slkp0t", "address": "$9444", "text": "CCAMEL - control the camel. Joystick port 2 at $DC00, bits active LOW: $01 up, $02 down, $04 left, $08 right, $10 fire.\n\nThe camel is object slot 0: objXPos, objYPos, objFieldFB0 and sprShape all at index 0. Bit 7 of objFieldFB0 is the facing flag.\n\nRuns on a divider - DEC camelTick ($34), and only acts when it reaches zero, reloading $80. So the camel moves once every 128 ticks of whatever drives the main loop, which is what makes it walk rather than fly.\n\nGround behaviour:\n  right  -> INC objXPos, clamped at $90\n  left   -> face left (objFieldFB0 = $80, shape $C0), refused at objXPos $11\n  up     -> jump: camelMode = 1, jumpVelocity = $0F, jumpHeldRight remembers whether right was also held, shape $CD\n  down   -> camelMode = 2, shape $CE, refused at the left limit\nNeither left nor right, or the right limit reached, falls into $9492 which clears the facing flag and JMPs to $C000 - the keyboard check.\n\ncamelMode ($33) is the state machine: 0 walking, 1 rising, 2 ducking, 5 landing. Non-zero sends this routine to the physics at $94F6 instead of reading the stick, so you cannot steer while airborne except by the direction you were already holding." },
        { "id": "cmt_d12bcd", "address": "$9468", "text": "Inside CCAMEL (sub_9444, the player routine, called every frame): LDA $46; BEQ normal-joystick-handling; else JMP loc_9D59 (zone advance). $46 is stashed with the held key by sub_9744 ($9751) only when cheat mode ($5E) is on and a creature spawn just succeeded. $46 is cleared back to 0 by loc_9699 as part of the new zones setup (LDA #$00/STA $46 at $96A5-96A7), so this fires once per trigger, not every frame. Net effect: cheat mode + holding any key at a spawn moment skips straight to the next zone. Traced end to end; not run live yet." },
        { "id": "cmt_caijd5", "address": "$94F6", "text": "Jump physics, reached when camelMode is not zero.\n\nMode 1, rising and falling: objYPos changes by jumpVelocity ($39), treated as SIGNED - bit 7 set means add the complement instead of subtracting, which is how the same counter serves both halves of the arc. jumpVelocity is decremented every step, so it counts $0F, $0E ... $00, $FF ... down to $F0, giving a symmetric parabola sixteen steps up and sixteen down. At $F0 the camel has landed: camelMode becomes 5, and voice 3 gets a short $0300 triangle burst with attack/decay $0C - the thud.\n\nIf jumpHeldRight was set the camel keeps drifting right for the whole jump, clamped at $90 like on the ground." },
        { "id": "cmt_11qj1c", "address": "$9565", "text": "4 bytes between the RTS-shaped byte at dat_9564 (itself read elsewhere as a data table via LDA dat_9564,X) and the real code resuming at $9569 (LDX $39, reached directly, not by fallthrough). Forcing decode as code produced an overlap warning -- reverted. Bytes: CE CF CF CE." },
        { "id": "cmt_griq39", "address": "$9565", "text": "Forcing decode of a small undecoded gap between two already-decoded routines; bytes looked like plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_u3nnbd", "address": "$9565", "text": "Four sprite shapes for the landing squash, CE CF CF CE, indexed 1-based by jumpVelocity counting 4 down to 1. The base operand is $9564, which is the RTS of the routine above - the same one-below-the-table trick the star seed tables use at $8A0D." },
        { "id": "cmt_qjfglm", "address": "$95B9", "text": "beryl (amber·s block, comment only):\n\nTHE PLAYER·S SHOT — amber·s BULITZ from the leftover source.\n\n$3C counts down from 12, so this runs once every twelve calls. $95CB tests $DC00 bit 4 (fire) and then bits 0-3 for a direction; all four set means the stick is centred and nothing happens. The direction is latched in $3B, and it is only accepted when $33 (the player action) is 0, 1 or 5.\n\nFiring allocates virtual sprite slot 1 through sub_92F6 and places it at objXPos + $13, objYPos + 2 — an offset from the PLAYER, which is slot 0 — then plays a pulse-wave blip on voice 1 and sets $3D.\n\nloc_962E flies it: bit 0 of $3B moves it up two, bit 1 down two, bit 2 left one, bit 3 right one, every frame, in whatever direction the stick was held when it was fired. $9658 kills it at X = $06 or $B0 and at Y = $10, $F8 or $0F.\n\nSo the shot is fired in eight directions and travels in a straight line. Note it moves TWO pixels vertically and ONE horizontally, which makes the diagonals steep rather than 45 degrees." },
        { "id": "cmt_x44vtv", "address": "$95B9", "text": "BULITZ - the bullet. There is exactly ONE, object slot 1, and $3B bulletDirection is both the flag that it exists and the direction it travels.\n\nFiring: on a $0C-tick repeat delay ($3C). Requires fire down AND at least one direction down - the CMP #$0F test rejects fire with the stick centred, so this game has no straight-ahead shot, only eight-way. The direction bits are saved whole into $3B and the bullet then moves by them for its whole life; you cannot steer it.\n\nIt also refuses to fire unless camelMode is 0, 1 or 5 - so you can shoot while running, rising or landing, but not while ducking (mode 2).\n\nThe bullet starts at the camel position plus ($13, $02) and moves 2 pixels vertically or 1 horizontally per step, which makes diagonals steeper than 45 degrees.\n\nIt dies at $9677 when it reaches X $06 or $B0, or Y $10, $0F, $F8 or $F9. Two pairs of Y limits because the vertical step is 2 and the counter can pass a limit on either parity - checking one value would let a bullet escape half the time. That is a real detail, not redundancy.\n\nSound: a falling-pitch pulse on voice 1, but only if no tune is playing ($1A) - so in-game the laser and the music share voice 1 and the music wins." },
        { "id": "cmt_hqinru", "address": "$9680", "text": "The laser sound decay. Reuses voice1NoteTicks ($17) as the pitch: decrement it, write it straight to $D401 as the frequency high byte, and gate the voice off when it reaches zero. So the shot is a downward sweep from $40 to $00 over 64 calls. $3D is the flag that this is running.\n\nWorth noting because it is why the music player and the shot fight over voice 1: they use the same zero page byte for two different purposes." },
        { "id": "cmt_tswyeb", "address": "$9699", "text": "Copy this zone template block into the working tables: 152 bytes from ($3E),Y - which StartZone has pointed at $6700 + 200*zoneNumber - into $1DA0 upwards. Every tmpl* array is a slice of this one copy." },
        { "id": "cmt_ih1c2q", "address": "$96D2", "text": "THE CHEAT CODE, and it spells GOATS.\n\ncheatKeySequence ($96F7) is five keyboard matrix scan codes: $1A $26 $0A $16 $0D = G, O, A, T, S. The routine reads them as two overlapping tables, which is why the data looks one byte short of what the code implies:\n\n  CMP cheatKeySequence,X     ($96F7 + progress) - the key already accepted: still held, do nothing\n  CMP cheatKeySequence+1,X   ($96F8 + progress) - the next key wanted: accept it, INC cheatProgress\n  anything else              - cheatProgress = 0, start again\n\ncheatProgress ($5F) runs 0-4 and when it reaches 4 the accumulator still holds 4, so STA cheatFlag ($5E) sets cheat mode to a non-zero value and jumps to $9406. From there ShowCheatModeBanner ($57D1) puts \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" on the message line and CheckPauseKey ($5817) returns without pausing.\n\nThe letters were decoded against the standard C64 keyboard matrix, the same table that proves the game charset at $56AE: 26=G, 38=O, 10=A, 22=T, 13=S. Five for five, in that order, in a Jeff Minter game about camels.\n\n(basalt, writing inside amber block - say the word and I will leave the naming to you.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_nld0ed", "address": "$96D2", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_p4g19u", "address": "$96D2", "text": "What cheat mode actually does, exhaustively checked: only 5 sites in the whole program read $5E. $57D1 draws the CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE banner. $581A (sub_5817) makes the attract-mode caption cycler stop overwriting the message line once cheat mode is on. $9751 (sub_9744, creature-spawn path) conditionally stashes the currently-held key's matrix code into scratch byte $46 if cheat mode is on and a key is held - inconclusive: $46 is reused by 8+ unrelated routines and no confirmed consumer of this particular write was found. No confirmed effect on lives, invulnerability, zone selection, or anything else. As far as this project can show, the cheat is cosmetic: it changes what the message line says and nothing else." },
        { "id": "cmt_vrri2m", "address": "$96D2", "text": "Reads LSTX ($C5), the KERNAL last-key-matrix-code. Walks a 5-entry table at $96F7 (matrix codes for G O A T S, verified against the standard C64 keyboard matrix) using an index/counter in $5F: table[X] is the key already accepted, table[X+1] is the key wanted next. A wrong key resets the counter. On the 5th correct key it sets $5E (cheat flag) and tail-jumps into sub_9406 (the message-line clear/redraw routine used all over the game), which is why typing G-O-A-T-S during play redraws the message line as CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE." },
        { "id": "cmt_ll541n", "address": "$96F7", "text": "Read by sub_96D2 (CMP dat_96F7,X then CMP dat_96F8,X, X from zp $5F) as two overlapping bytewise arrays one offset apart -- looks like a per-index (lo,hi) range test, e.g. is LSTX ($C5, the last key scanned) between dat_96F7[X] and dat_96F8[X]. dat_96F8 overlaps already-decoded code one byte to the right, so its own extent was not separately claimed." },
        { "id": "cmt_mj9vgf", "address": "$96FC", "text": "SPAWN TICK, run once per pass of the main loop.\n\nFirst a timer: $43 counts down and reloads with 12 minus twice loopNumber, so the whole spawn system runs FASTER on later loops of the game - that is the only difficulty knob outside the zone data.\n\nThen the pause key check, the camel colour fade, and finally the spawn scan: for each of the eight object types, decrement typeSpawnTimer[type] and when it hits zero call SpawnOneOfType. So the zone template byte tmplSpawnInterval controls how often each creature appears, per type, per zone.\n\nEnds by jumping to $98A9, which runs the per-object update over slots 2-15." },
        { "id": "cmt_1iobog", "address": "$9735", "text": "LDX #$02 (slots 0-1 reserved); loop: LDA dat_1F80,X (slot occupied flag); BEQ found (X=free slot); INX; CPX $42 (max live slots, loaded per-zone from zoneTable record+$9B=maxLiveSlots); BNE loop; else LDX #$FF (no free slot). Matches the prior-run memos description of the sprite multiplexers dead search-limit routine, now with the limits data source traced." },
        { "id": "cmt_elepiy", "address": "$9735", "text": "Searches object slots X=2.. up to $42 (exclusive) for the first with dat_1F80,X==0 (free); returns X, or $FF if none found. $42 is loaded per-zone from the zone record scalar field (record+$9B, see loc_9CB5/sub_9CFB) - 8 in 41 of the 42 zones, 6 in zone 6 (one confirmed by reading the code, matching reader one's data-side finding independently). Slots 0/1 are skipped (reserved, presumably player + bullet). Since this loop never runs past $42, and $42 never exceeds 8 across the whole zone table, object slots 8-15 (a second sprite bank) are never allocated by this path -- structural confirmation of the memo's dead sprite-multiplexer claim." },
        { "id": "cmt_ta7r58", "address": "$9735", "text": "beryl: FREE SLOT SEARCH, and the bounds are the object model.\n\nLDX #$02, scan sprInUse,X for a zero, stop at CPX $42. $42 comes from offset +155 of the zone record and is 8 in 41 of the 42 zones (6 in zone 6, HAVEN·T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE).\n\nSo creatures occupy virtual sprite slots 2 to 7 — SIX of them at once — because slot 0 is the player camel and slot 1 is its shot. Returning $FF means the wave is full and the spawn is dropped, which is why tmplSpawnInterval is a rate rather than a schedule." },
        { "id": "cmt_cumoae", "address": "$9772", "text": "Entry: Y = creature type (0-7), X = live-slot base. Copies ~13 fields from the current zones template block ($1DA0+, one 8-byte column per field, Y-indexed) into the live slot at X, including spriteFirst/spriteLast (offsets $10/$18 in the 152-byte template = zoneTable record +$10/+$18) and the lifetime reload (record +$80). Reached three ways in this program: a fresh spawn, aging out (sub_9A39, tail-jumps here with the new type in Y), and (per the prior-run memo, not independently re-checked by me) being shot. All land in the same call -- an explosion is just another creature type (see nextType/$FF, lifetime=12, sprite $EC-$EE on the type-7 column of every zone I sampled)." },
        { "id": "cmt_x96dv8", "address": "$9772", "text": "X = target object slot, Y = template/type index (from sub_C061 -> sub_9DBB). Copies one byte from each of ~10 per-type template arrays (dat_1DA8,Y / dat_1DB0,Y / dat_1DB8,Y / dat_1DC0,Y / dat_1DE0,Y / dat_1DE8,Y / dat_1DF0,Y / dat_1DF8,Y / dat_1E18,Y / dat_1E20,Y) into the matching per-instance arrays at offset X (dat_1FB0/dat_1D30+dat_1F70/dat_1D40/dat_1D50/dat_1D60+dat_1E70/dat_1D70+dat_1E80/dat_1D80/dat_1D90/dat_1F60/dat_1E90), then stores Y itself into obj_type,X. The template tables are separate parallel byte arrays (not one interleaved record), so add_type (fields-within-one-block) does not fit this shape -- documenting the pairing in these comments is the workaround." },
        { "id": "cmt_9wp3vf", "address": "$985E", "text": "SPAWN MODE 3: place the new object RELATIVE TO ANOTHER ONE.\n\ntmplSpawnMode is 0 for a fixed position and $10 for random-if-zero. Anything else lands here, and its low nibble minus one is a creature TYPE: scan slots 2 to 15 for a live object of that type, and if one is found put the new object at that object·s position plus tmplSpawnX and tmplSpawnY.\n\nThat is how a creature spawns something at itself — an egg under a bird, a bomb under a ship — without anything having to pass a position.\n\nAND IF THERE IS NO SUCH OBJECT IT DOES PLA PLA RTS at $9878, discarding its own return address and returning to its caller·s caller. That cancels the whole spawn two levels up rather than creating something in the wrong place. Worth knowing before trusting any call graph through here: PlaceNewObject·s caller does not always get control back." },
        { "id": "cmt_37ex64", "address": "$98A9", "text": "THE OBJECT UPDATE LOOP: slots 2 to 15, skipping any whose objMoveType is zero, calling UpdateOneObject on the rest.\n\nNote the range. FindFreeObjectSlot only ever allocates in 2 to $42-1, which is 2 to 7 in 41 of the 42 zones, so spawned creatures live in six slots — 0 is the player camel and 1 is its shot. But this loop runs to 15, which is the full sixteen the sprite multiplexer supports, so slots 8-15 are driven by something other than the spawner and appear on the odd-numbered frames." },
        { "id": "cmt_pabrs9", "address": "$98BD", "text": "THE ANIMATION STEPPER, and it names four object fields at once.\n\n  objAnimFirst $1D30  first frame\n  objAnimLast  $1D40  the frame to stop or turn at\n  objAnimMode  $1D50  bit 6 = ping-pong, bit 7 = currently running backwards, low 6 bits = the frame delay\n  objAnimDelay $1D20  the countdown, reloaded from tmplAnimMode AND #$3F\n\nsprShape,X is the live frame — the same byte the multiplexer writes into $07F8+n, so animating an object is literally counting its sprite pointer up.\n\nThree behaviours:\n- objAnimFirst equals objAnimLast: nothing moves, a still sprite.\n- bit 6 clear: INC sprShape until it reaches objAnimLast, then snap back to objAnimFirst. A loop.\n- bit 6 set: INC to objAnimLast, then set bit 7 and DEC back to objAnimFirst, then clear it. A ping-pong.\n\nUpdateOneObject at $9910 is the per-object tick: count objAnimDelay down, animate, move by type, age, draw." },
        { "id": "cmt_6rqald", "address": "$9933", "text": "Low/high byte pairs (interleaved: dat_9933,Y = lo, dat_9934,Y = hi), read by sub_993A: Y = 2 * (dat_1FB0,X AND $7F) -- a per-object value with its top bit masked off, doubled to a word index. Result stored to $02/$03, then JMP ($0002) at $994C. 3 entries decode to real routines: Y=0 -> $9939, Y=2 -> $994F, Y=4 -> $99C0 -- so this is a behaviour dispatch: an object's low-7-bits-of-dat_1FB0 value selects which of (at least) 3 update routines runs for it. I had first force-decoded these same 6 bytes as code (AND/STA/RTS) before finding the real reader at $9933 -- reverted." },
        { "id": "cmt_fpv6oa", "address": "$9933", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_sd37n3", "address": "$9933", "text": "Three movement handlers as ordinary lo/hi pairs. sub_993A doubles the index and reads $9933,Y for the low byte and $9934,Y for the high one.\n\n  entry 0 -> $9939, which is the $60 IMMEDIATELY AFTER this table. A byte doubling as an RTS: movement type 0 does nothing.\n  entry 1 -> $994F  MoveObjectLinear\n  entry 2 -> $99C0  MoveObjectAccelerating\n\nThe index is objMoveType,X AND #$7F, so bit 7 of that byte is free for something else — and the player uses it for facing ($9492 and $94A3 write $00 and $80 there).\n\n$994C is JMP ($0002): the walk cannot follow a vector, which is why 103 instructions below it were invisible. $02/$03 is the print-column scratch pair being borrowed as a jump vector. Marking the two targets recovered them all." },
        { "id": "cmt_3m6b6p", "address": "$994F", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_n12ppq", "address": "$994F", "text": "MOVEMENT HANDLER 1: constant speed, expressed as a COUNTDOWN rather than a rate.\n\nX and Y are handled identically. Take objSpeedX,X; if it is zero do nothing. Otherwise split it into a sign (bit 7) and a magnitude (bits 0-6), decrement the magnitude, and put it back with the sign restored. When the magnitude reaches zero, reload it from objBaseSpeedX,X and move objXPos,X by objField80,X — adding when bit 7 of the reload value is clear, subtracting when it is set.\n\nSo the byte is a delay, not a velocity: a LARGER value means a SLOWER object. objBaseSpeedX / objBaseSpeedY hold the reload and the direction, and objField80 is the step in pixels.\n\nThat is what makes loc_5049 work. It sets or clears bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX,X and objBaseSpeedY,X by comparing this object against slot 0, which is the player — one bit each, and the creature turns to face you." },
        { "id": "cmt_hmketd", "address": "$99C0", "text": "MOVEMENT HANDLER 2: ballistic, and it reads the SAME fields differently. Worth knowing before naming anything in the object arrays: there is no single meaning for objSpeedX.\n\nHorizontal: the LOW NIBBLE of objSpeedX,X is a step in pixels, applied every frame, added or subtracted on bit 7. No countdown.\n\nVertical: objSpeedY,X is a signed velocity that is DECREMENTED every frame, which is gravity. While it is positive the object rises by that much; once bit 7 is set the object falls, one pixel at a time, up to $FF-value steps.\n\nThe fall stops at objYPos = $E0, the ground line, and then one of three things happens on objField80,X:\n  zero          -> objSpeedY = #$10, a fixed bounce\n  bit 7 set     -> $9A29 mixes sub_9856 with objField80, masks to 7 bits and adds 4, a varying bounce\n  otherwise     -> DEC objField80,X, then LDY objField80,X and JMP sub_9772, which RESPAWNS THIS SLOT as a different template. So objField80 is a countdown of bounces, and when it runs out the thing turns into something else.\n\nThat last branch is the most interesting thing in the object system: a creature can metamorphose on landing." },
        { "id": "cmt_iq39zb", "address": "$99C0", "text": "Forcing decode of a small undecoded gap between two already-decoded routines; bytes looked like plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_2eqmar", "address": "$9A39", "text": "Per-frame lifetime countdown for a live creature slot at X. If dat_1E90,X (lifetime) is 0, does nothing (immortal at this stage). Otherwise decrements it; when it reaches 0: Y = dat_1D10,X (this slots creature type), reload dat_1E90,X from the template lifetime column ($1DA0+$80,Y = zoneTable record+$80), then read nextType from $1DA0+$38,Y = zoneTable record+$38. If nextType is $FF the slot is freed (JMP sub_9328). Otherwise Y=nextType and JMP sub_9772 (SpawnCreature) -- the slot respawns in place as the new type. Verified with find_instructions on $1E90 and $1DD8 ($1DA0+$38): both addresses have exactly the reads/writes this description claims and no others." },
        { "id": "cmt_9i4vkn", "address": "$9A39", "text": "OBJECT LIFETIME, and this is the metamorphosis mechanic.\n\nobjLifetime,X counts down every tick. At zero it is reloaded from tmplLifetime,Y and the object CHANGES INTO SOMETHING ELSE: $9A4F reads $1DD8,Y — the ninth template table, which renders here as spriteSet1 + $05D8 only because the label at $1800 carries a 2K extent that reaches this far — and\n\n  $FF        -> sub_9328, free the slot. The thing dies.\n  anything else -> sub_9772 with that as the new type. The thing becomes it.\n\nSo $1DD8 is tmplNextType and the creature templates form a CHAIN: each type says what it turns into and how long it has first. Together with the bounce-countdown respawn at $9A20 that is two independent routes from one creature to another, and it is the mechanic that makes a Minter attack wave keep changing shape while you watch." },
        { "id": "cmt_pv0g82", "address": "$9A5F", "text": "Kill an object that has left the play area. X and Y are treated as sign-magnitude - bit 7 is the sign and the low seven bits the value - so the limits are: X below 5 on the positive side or below $33 on the negative side, Y below $10 positive or $70 or more negative. Anything outside runs the type-specific cleanup at $9C51 and frees the sprite.\n\nSign-magnitude rather than two-complement is unusual and it is consistent across the object system: objSpeedX and objSpeedY use the same convention at $994F, where the low seven bits are a countdown and bit 7 the direction." },
        { "id": "cmt_acnomy", "address": "$9A9C", "text": "COLLISION, and there are TWO tests here with different mechanisms - which is the interesting part.\n\nBULLET vs this object ($9AC2-$9B12): pure coordinates. objX - bulletX - 4 must come out negative and, complemented, below $18; same in Y. So a 24x24 box, tested in software for every object every tick.\n\nCAMEL vs this object ($9B1B-$9B6B): the VIC hardware collision register. $44 is $D01E latched once per frame at $8C01 (reading it clears it, so it can only be read in one place), ANDed with this object sprite bit and with bit 0, the camel sprite. Then confirmed by a coordinate box, $20 wide this time.\n\nWHY THE DIFFERENCE, and this is a finding rather than a guess: the bullet test HAS the hardware gate and it is stubbed out. $9AB3 is LDA #$FF / AND #$02 and $9ABC is LDA #$FF / AND printChar - both immediate $FF, so both always pass. Put $A5 $44 in place of $A9 $FF and they become exactly the shape of the camel test at $9B1B: latch AND sprite-bit. Two bytes, twice, and the routine is symmetrical.\n\nThat is a deliberate disable, and the reason is visible elsewhere in the program: the multiplexer at $925F draws objects 8-15 on alternate frames, so a sprite that is not displayed this frame registers no hardware collision. A bullet that misses half the time is not acceptable; a creature that is hit a frame late is. So the bullet was moved to software and the dead gate left in place.\n\nOn a hit: score by type, then the object becomes tmplTypeWhenShot rather than simply dying, and the bullet is freed. On a camel collision: explode for tmplExplodeLength steps of neutronium and become tmplTypeWhenExploded. Nothing in this game is just destroyed - it turns into the next thing." },
        { "id": "cmt_fqjzg6", "address": "$9B94", "text": "SCORING. Y is the object type, and the points come from tmplScoreValue at $1E28 - one byte per type, part of the zone record, so the same creature is worth different amounts in different zones.\n\nThe byte is packed: low nibble = which score digit, high nibble = how many times to add one to it. scoreField is $040D and the seven digits are $040E-$0414, so digit 7 is units, 6 is tens, 5 is hundreds. $15 is therefore one hundred, $56 is fifty, $55 is five hundred. Carry is propagated by hand at $9BCD: a digit passing $3A wraps to $30 and the loop moves one place left.\n\nThe score is kept in ASCII digits in screen memory and nowhere else - there is no binary copy anywhere. Same design as the neutronium bar.\n\nIt also fires the hit sound: voice 2 gated on with attack/decay $0A, and $41 set to 4 so $9B6D sweeps it. A score value of 0 or $FF scores nothing and makes no noise, which is how a zone marks a creature as harmless or as scenery." },
        { "id": "cmt_nf8ht8", "address": "$9BE9", "text": "Explosion. tmplExplodeLength at $1E30 - again per type, per zone - gives the number of DrainNeutronium calls the explosion runs for, so an explosion COSTS FUEL. Then voice 3 is gated with frequency high $08 for the bang.\n\nIt borrows printColour ($05) as the loop counter and printChar ($04) to save X, which is why this routine appears to touch the print variables without printing anything." },
        { "id": "cmt_d989xh", "address": "$9C0C", "text": "Drain one step of neutronium.\n\nINC the current bar cell; when it reaches $50 the segment is used up, so blank it to $20 and DEC neutroniumLeft. The two instructions that do that, at $9C12 and $9C22, are the ones $C023 and $C046 rewrite - see the comment there.\n\nWhen neutroniumLeft reaches zero the camel is out of fuel, and which of two things happens depends on zoneOverFlag: $0F means this is the deliberate end-of-zone drain, so set it to $FF and let EmptyNeutroniumBar see that and stop; anything else means the player has run out during play, so titleFlashCounter goes to 1 and that is what starts the death sequence.\n\nEvery three drains ($4B) it steps $4C down through neutroniumColourRamp at $9C4A - 00 0C 0E 0C 0A 09 08 - into camelColourTarget ($40), which $9B6D then fades the camel sprite towards. So the camel visibly changes colour as its fuel runs down, and the ramp being read backwards is why the sequence looks reversed in the data." },
        { "id": "cmt_a2xy0y", "address": "$9C4A", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_b367fb", "address": "$9C4A", "text": "7-byte gap before sub_9C51. Bytes 00 0C 0E 0C 0A 09 08. Not yet traced to a reader." },
        { "id": "cmt_2gqnjx", "address": "$9CB5", "text": "Reads the zone records 8 scalar bytes (record+$98..$9F) in order via repeated JSR sub_9CFB, storing to ZP $53,$52,$51,$42(=maxLiveSlots, the FindFreeObjectSlot bound),$02(=zoneParam02_maybe),SPMC0($D025),SPMC1($D026),... -- confirms which zoneTable scalar field feeds which live variable." },
        { "id": "cmt_s5rd13", "address": "$9CB5", "text": "basalt: note from outside this block, because it explains something in mine.\n\nThis reads a stream of parameter bytes through sub_9CFB (LDA ($3E),Y / INY) and distributes them: $53, $52, $51, $42, then $02, then SPMC0, SPMC1, one more byte, then 40 characters into messageLine-1 ($058F), then JSR CopyCharSetToRAM ($5007).\n\nSo ($3E) points at a per-zone descriptor: sprite multicolours, a 40-character banner, and a character set index. CopyCharSetToRAM takes that index in $02 and moves 2K from charSetSourceTable ($502F) into $0800-$0FFF. The six slots in that table are $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800 - the last two of which are program code, so either they are never selected or my reading of the interleaved lo/hi table is wrong. Whoever owns $3E/$3F can settle it by reading the descriptor data." },
        { "id": "cmt_5ysvgd", "address": "$9CFF", "text": "The message line countdown, called once per frame from CheckPauseKey ($5817).\n\nmessageTickDivider ($4E) counts $60 frames; on each expiry it ticks TickZoneDistance ($9D2C) and then, if messageTimer ($4F) is non-zero, decrements it and recolours the whole message line from messageColourCycle ($9D21) = black, blue, red, purple, green, cyan, yellow, white - indexed by the timer, so the line cycles through seven colours over about seven seconds. When the timer reaches zero it jumps to ClearMessageLine.\n\nSo every banner in the game - BEASTS REMAINING, STAND BY YOUR BEAST, CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE, the wave name - is written to $0590 and then colour-cycled and wiped by this one routine." },
        { "id": "cmt_osxfxt", "address": "$9D21", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_s14e09", "address": "$9D21", "text": "8-byte gap between two routines. First byte reads as BRK if force-decoded as code (a mis-decode I made and reverted); bytes are 00 06 02 04 05 03 07 01 -- the last 7 look like a permutation of 1..7. Not yet traced to a reader (no LDA tbl,X found near sub_9D2C/loc_9D29)." },
        { "id": "cmt_u2gyq1", "address": "$9D21", "text": "Eight colours - 00 06 02 04 05 03 07 01 - cycled into messageLineColour ($D990) by $9D13, indexed by $4F counting down. Entry 0 is black, so the message fades to invisible on the last step before $9D29 clears the line." },
        { "id": "cmt_bwy0pc", "address": "$9D2C", "text": "Count down \"NEXT ZONE nn KM\" on the status line. zoneKmTickDivider ($47) divides by $10; each expiry decrements the ones digit at $0422, borrowing into the tens at $0421 when it passes $2F (below screen-code 0), and when both reach 0 the zone is complete." },
        { "id": "cmt_p88mt0", "address": "$9D59", "text": "ZONE COMPLETE. Silences all three SID voices, waits for every active sprite to walk off screen (the loop at $9D6E pushes each objXPos up until it wraps and then frees the sprite), then advances the zone.\n\n$9D9C-$9DB3 is where the game length is decided: INC zoneNumber, and if it reaches #$2A = 42 the player has finished all 42 zones - show WELL DONE, bump loopNumber ($50), cap it at 5, and set the zone back to 0 so the game loops harder. That is the only place either counter changes, so 42 zones per loop and 6 loops of difficulty is the whole progression.\n\nNote $9DB5: LDX #$F8 / TXS resets the stack pointer before jumping to $937B, so this never returns to whoever called it. Anything reasoning about the call graph through here is wrong." },
        { "id": "cmt_djzny1", "address": "$9DBB", "text": "Set the VIC per-sprite flag bits for one object type. X is the sprite number, Y the object type.\n\nBuilds 1<<(X AND 7) into printColour ($05) and 1<<Y into $06, then tests $06 against three per-type masks - $53 multicolour, $51 X-expand, $52 Y-expand - and ORs the sprite bit into $D01C, $D01D and $D017 respectively. So the zone data says which object TYPES are multicoloured or expanded, and this turns that into the per-SPRITE register bits at the moment a sprite is allocated.\n\nWorth comparing with the leftover source at $5870, which contains the same idea written differently:\n  SSET TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 / TANGE ROL A / DEY / BNE TANGE\nThat version uses SEC+ROL and this one CLC+ASL, so it is a rewrite rather than the same code - but SSET is what the author called this." },
        { "id": "cmt_7ngi5a", "address": "$9E0A", "text": "END OF ZONE: empty the bar, then fill it again. This is the bonus sequence.\n\nFirst half ($9E10): zoneOverFlag = $0F, then call DrainNeutronium over and over with a falling siren on voice 1 ($00 down to $C0 in the frequency high byte) until the flag turns $FF, which DrainNeutronium does when the last segment goes. printRow counts how many drains it took - that is the bonus.\n\nSecond half ($9E52 onwards): double that count, then refill the bar from cell 1 upward with a rising siren, using the self-modified instructions at $9E68 and $9EB8.\n\nSo the reward for finishing a zone with fuel left is measured by emptying the bar and counting, which is exactly the kind of thing you do when the bar is the only place the number is kept." },
        { "id": "cmt_tcjb6c", "address": "$9E6B", "text": "Refill the bar, second half of the end-of-zone sequence. StepBarCellDown at $9EB6 walks the current cell down from $4F towards $47; when it gets there the cell is full, so it steps to the next one and the camel colour walks back UP the ramp at $9C4A - the inverse of the drain. It stops either at cell $13 (bar full, and $9EEA pulls the index back) or when printRow, the doubled bonus count, runs out.\n\nA rising siren on voice 1 runs alongside, $00 up to $40, which is exactly the falling siren of the drain played backwards." },
        { "id": "cmt_uvkvto", "address": "$9EFD", "text": "Add to the score without going through a type. PHA PHA then JMP into the middle of AddScoreForType at $9BCB, which is the point after that routine has pushed Y and X - so the two dummy pushes exist only to balance the two pulls at $9BE4. X is the digit column and Y the number of ones to add.\n\nIts one caller, $9E22, passes X = 6, Y = 3 on every step of the end-of-zone drain. Digit 6 is the tens column, so finishing a zone pays THIRTY POINTS per step of neutronium left. That is the entire end-of-level bonus, and this is where the number lives." },
        { "id": "cmt_q3ulvt", "address": "$9F02", "text": "Move type 2: give this object the direction the player last fired in. bulletDirection ($3B) holds the raw joystick bits from the shot, and they become speed $81 (negative 1) or $01 in X and Y. Reached from the type dispatch at $9C9F.\n\nSo a zone can declare a creature that chases the direction of your last shot. That is not something a static table could express, which is presumably why it is a code case rather than a data field." },
        { "id": "cmt_4mgut5", "address": "$9F4C", "text": "THE CAMEL DIES. Reached from $9465 when titleFlashCounter says the neutronium ran out.\n\nThe animation is three loops, each of them DelayXY-paced so it plays at a watchable speed regardless of what the raster is doing:\n  $9F67  shape $CD, objYPos[0] counted DOWN to $80  - the camel rises off the top of the screen\n  $9F7E  shape $9C, objYPos[0] counted UP to $C8    - it comes back down\n  $9F90  objXPos[0] counted down to zero            - it walks off to the left\nEvery sprite but the camel is freed first ($9F53 over slots 1-7).\n\nThen $9F9D: reset camelMode, the jump state and the move type, refill the neutronium bar to eighteen full cells, reset the colour ramp, and DEC beastsRemaining ($54). If any beast is left, show the count, swap players and start the zone again at $93AB. If not, this is game over: check the high score table and JMP $C082, which writes it to disk and restarts the game.\n\nSo a life costs one beast and always restarts the CURRENT zone with a full bar - there is no progress penalty beyond the beast." },
        { "id": "cmt_7qg3xu", "address": "$9FEF", "text": "Seventeen bytes between the last routine and $A000, where BASIC ROM takes over. 90 00 FF 10 00 00 00 FF 00 80 00 18 43 42 4F 4F 16 - the tail reads CBOO in PETSCII, so this is most likely residue from the build machine like the assembler source at $5870, not data. Nothing references it and no code reaches it. I cannot tell what it was." },
        { "id": "cmt_rt69ks", "address": "$9FEF", "text": "Sampled the whole span with read_bytes in 8192-byte chunks: $A001-$BFFF (8191 bytes) is uniformly zero. Only $9FEF-$A000 (18 bytes: 90 00 FF 10 00 00 00 FF 00 80 00 18 43 42 4F 4F 16 CD) is non-zero and I have not traced what wrote it or reads it -- code ends cleanly just before, at $9FEC (JMP loc_93AB), so this is not a decode gap, it is genuinely past the end of anything the disassembly reaches. Guess: reserved/scratch RAM (a level or scratch buffer built at runtime) between the game code and the $C000 loader tail, deliberately left zero at rest -- not confirmed by any write site found so far." },
        { "id": "cmt_0cri9j", "address": "$A000", "text": "8192 bytes of zero in the image, and never read as RAM.\n\nNothing in this program writes $00 or $01, so the 6510 port stays at its power-on $37 and BASIC ROM is banked in across $A000-$BFFF. Every read of this range — NextRandom at $8D21, and TitleColourAndSoundStep at $50BD — gets ROM, not these bytes.\n\nDeclared data so it stops showing as unexplained. It is explained: it is a window onto ROM that this project cannot model, because a layer supplying an address always wins and nothing has loaded a KERNAL or BASIC image." },
        { "id": "cmt_clhsr5", "address": "$C000", "text": "CMP #$50 twice in a row, and the second is dead: if the first BEQ did not take, A is unchanged, so the second compare has the same result and its BEQ cannot take either. Reads as a leftover from an edit, probably two different key codes once. Live behaviour: $DC01 == $50 goes to $C012, anything else JMP $94AF." },
        { "id": "cmt_phb85o", "address": "$C000", "text": "Reads CIA1PRB ($DC01) raw (not via KERNAL scan) and CMPs it against #$50 twice in a row with no instruction in between that could change A. The first CMP/BEQ is live (-> loc_C012, normal start). The second CMP/BEQ (-> loc_C00E, which sets zone counter $58 to #$29=41 decimal, i.e. jump straight to the LAST zone, zone index 41 = REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS, verified by reading its name at $87A8) can never be taken: A cannot differ between two CMP #$50s with nothing in between. Whatever the second key code was meant to be, it was never filled in (or was edited to match the first by mistake). A skip-to-the-last-zone cheat, dead on arrival." },
        { "id": "cmt_0rvvcq", "address": "$C00E", "text": "LDA #$29 (41 decimal); STA $58 -- $58 is the zone-index variable consumed by the NEXINI stepping loop at $93E0-$93F2. Only reachable via the second, impossible CMP #$50 in CheckZone41Cheat at $C000." },
        { "id": "cmt_9uuekj", "address": "$C023", "text": "Self-modifying code: patches OPCODE bytes into seven sites in the $9C00-$9FFF routines, then runs them.\n\n  #$C6 = DEC zp   -> $9C22, $9EEA, $9FBB\n  #$99 = STA abs,Y -> $9E68\n  #$9D = STA abs,X -> $9FB0\n  #$DE = DEC abs,X -> $9EB8\n  #$FE = INC abs,X -> $9C12\n\nEvery one of those is a WRITE or a decrement. $C046 is the exact mirror and pokes LDA in the same seven slots. So the code at $9DBB/$9E0A is one routine driven in two modes, and a static reading of those seven addresses is reading whichever opcode was poked last.\n\nEvidence: byte-for-byte mirror pair, and marking $C046 a function decoded exactly the 10 instructions the byte reading predicted." },
        { "id": "cmt_gflbcm", "address": "$C023", "text": "Self-modifying patch: writes bytes into six sites in the sound and sprite code ($9C12, $9C22, $9E68, $9EB8, $9FB0, $9FBB) and then calls sub_9E0A. $C046 just below holds a second, unreferenced set of values for the same six addresses. Called from loc_9D59, which is on the $C000 path." },
        { "id": "cmt_o3erf6", "address": "$C023", "text": "Pokes 7 opcode bytes into code at $9C22/$9EEA/$9FBB (LDA zp $A5 -> DEC zp $C6), $9E68 (LDA abs,Y $B9 -> STA abs,Y $99), $9FB0 (LDA abs,X $BD -> STA abs,X $9D), $9EB8 (LDA abs,X $BD -> DEC abs,X $DE), $9C12 (LDA abs,X $BD -> INC abs,X $FE), then JSR sub_9E0A. Called from loc_9D59 at $9D99, which is the same zone-transition routine CheckZone41Cheat feeds into. Read directly with read_bytes: all 7 target addresses in the shipped image ALREADY hold the poked (write-mode) bytes, not the original LDA forms -- see $C046." },
        { "id": "cmt_zpkxom", "address": "$C023", "text": "Self-modifying-code patcher, called once from loc_9D59 ($9D99). Overwrites the OPCODE byte (not the operand) at 7 addresses inside other routines, always to a mutating form: dat_9C22/dat_9EEA/dat_9FBB <- $C6 (DEC zp, target stays $4A), dat_9E68 <- $99 (STA abs,Y, target dat_043C), dat_9FB0 <- $9D (STA abs,X, dat_043C), dat_9EB8 <- $DE (DEC abs,X, dat_043C), dat_9C12 <- $FE (INC abs,X, dat_043C). Paired with the opposite patcher at $C046 (patch_freeze_countdowns), which overwrites 5 of these 7 same addresses with non-mutating opcodes ($A5 LDA zp / $B9 LDA abs,Y / $BD LDA abs,X) -- same operand bytes, different opcode, so the surrounding instruction's addressing mode and operand are reused unchanged and only the verb (DEC/INC/STA -> LDA) flips. Guess: a shared piece of per-object update code is toggled between \"really tick this object's counters\" and \"just look, do not change\" -- e.g. attract-mode/demo vs real play, or a pause. Not confirmed by tracing what calls $C046 (no absolute-address caller found -- likely reached the same indirect way as the sprite routines at $8B5F)." },
        { "id": "cmt_4ho5jc", "address": "$C046", "text": "Opposite of patch_arm_countdowns ($C023): overwrites the same opcode bytes at dat_9C22/dat_9EEA/dat_9FBB/dat_9E68/dat_9C12 with non-mutating equivalents ($A5/$A5/$A5/$B9/$BD, all LDA). Does not touch dat_9FB0 or dat_9EB8, which patch_arm_countdowns also sets -- those two stay whatever they were last armed to. No absolute-address caller found for this routine." },
        { "id": "cmt_bq405j", "address": "$C046", "text": "READ-mode half of the pair with $C023: pokes #$A5 (LDA zp) into $9C22/$9EEA/$9FBB, #$B9 (LDA abs,Y) into $9E68, #$BD (LDA abs,X) into $9C12/$9EB8/$9FB0, then falls through into $C061 which calls $9DBB.\n\nWas sitting as data until marked a function; nothing references it absolutely, so it is reached by a computed or table dispatch that find_references cannot see." },
        { "id": "cmt_d18970", "address": "$C046", "text": "Forcing decode: undecoded span, checking whether it disassembles as plausible 6502." },
        { "id": "cmt_frfh9f", "address": "$C046", "text": "Not reached by any decode: 27 bytes with exactly the shape of sub_C023 above (LDA #$A5 / STA $9C22 / STA $9EEA / STA $9FBB / LDA #$B9 / STA $9E68 / LDA #$BD / STA $9C12 / STA $9EB8 / STA $9FB0). sub_C023 patches the same six addresses with a different set of values, so this is the other half of a pair - a second mode, or the undo - that nothing calls any more. Dead unless something reaches it through a computed jump." },
        { "id": "cmt_k8bvli", "address": "$C046", "text": "CORRECTION, with evidence: this is unreachable, and READ mode is never entered in this build.\n\n- find_references reports no inbound reference.\n- The two bytes $46 $C0 do not occur ANYWHERE in the 47K image, so no lo/hi pointer table and no pushed address can reach it either. Only arithmetic on a computed address could, and nothing here does that.\n- The loaded image already holds the WRITE opcodes at all seven patched sites: $9C12 is $FE (INC abs,X), $9C22 is $C6 (DEC zp), $9E68 is $99 (STA abs,Y), $9EB8 is $DE (DEC abs,X). So the program starts in WRITE mode.\n\nWhich makes sub_C023 a re-assertion of the state the program already ships in, and this routine the half that would undo it and never runs. Read as a debug or development switch left in - the same shape as the dead CMP #$50 at $C007 that disables the start-at-zone-41 branch just below.\n\nThat matters for anyone reading $9C0C, $9E0A or $9F90: those routines only ever run in WRITE mode, so the opcodes the listing shows are the ones that execute." },
        { "id": "cmt_qrxron", "address": "$C046", "text": "The mirror image of PokeZoneTransitionCode ($C023): pokes $A5/$B9/$BD back into the same 7 sites, undoing the poke. No inbound reference (find_references), and the byte pair 46 C0 (its own address, little-endian) occurs nowhere in the 47K image (find_bytes), so nothing -- code or a pointer table -- can reach it. As shipped the poke can only ever go forward: the game was saved to disk with the self-modified (write-mode) opcodes already in place at all 7 sites, and the code that would put them back can never run. The file is a photograph of a machine mid-session, not a pristine build." },
        { "id": "cmt_i8g7x4", "address": "$C061", "text": "One-instruction trampoline (JSR sub_9DBB; RTS) living in the small $C0xx block alongside the LOAD/SAVE and self-modifying-code patch routines -- this block seems to be used as a fixed, easy-to-JSR anchor point rather than calling sub_9DBB directly from spawn_object_from_template." },
        { "id": "cmt_f78kgd", "address": "$C065", "text": "Entry point. LOAD \"ATTACK MUTANT.HI\" from device 8 into memory at the address given by the file's own header, then JMP $8A3C (main entry)." },
        { "id": "cmt_p7zjmn", "address": "$C065", "text": "Load the high score table from disk.\nSETLFS 0,8,0 then SETNAM $10 chars at $C102 (\"ATTACK MUTANT.HI\"), then LOAD with A=0 (load) and X/Y = $5E00 as the destination, because secondary address 0 means \"ignore the load address in the file header\". So the table lives at $5E00, and the save at $C082 writes $5E00-$5ECF, which fixes its size at $D0 = 208 bytes.\nEvidence: the filename bytes at $C102, and the $FB/$FC pointer set to $5E00 at $C0A5 with X/Y = $5ED0 at $C0AD before JSR SAVE.\nNote the filename says ATTACK, not REVENGE. Either this build reuses the earlier game score file or the disk is mislabelled. Not resolved here." },
        { "id": "cmt_uuwabn", "address": "$C065", "text": "SETLFS device 8 secondary 0; SETNAM len $10 name at $C102 = \"ATTACK MUTANT.HI\" (skips the @: prefix at $C100, which is only meaningful for a save); LOAD with secondary 0 so the address in the file header wins. Filename proved by reading $C100 directly with read_bytes: 40 3A 41 54 54 41 43 4B 20 4D 55 54 41 4E 54 2E 48 49 = \"@:ATTACK MUTANT.HI\". The file is named after the 1983 prequel (Attack of the Mutant Camels), not this game." },
        { "id": "cmt_5mguwi", "address": "$C082", "text": "Cold start: SEI, IOINIT, RESTOR, sub_C0B9 (CINT-like init), border/bg to black, SETLFS/SETNAM/SAVE the same highscore file, then JMP $8A3C. Reached only via JMP from loc_9F90 ($9FE6), not from $0801/BASIC -- looks like the in-game reset/restart path (e.g. after game over or RESTORE) rather than the disk-load cold start." },
        { "id": "cmt_9w6td4", "address": "$C082", "text": "Game over: write the high score table back to disk, then restart at $8A3C.\nDoes a full KERNAL I/O reset first (IOINIT / RESTOR / CINT, and CINV back to $EA31) because the game runs its own raster IRQ and has taken the machine over; SAVE needs the KERNAL IRQ and screen back. Border and background are blanked to 0 so the restored screen is not seen.\nSaves $5E00-$5ECF under \"@:ATTACK MUTANT.HI\" - the @: prefix is the 1541 scratch-and-replace, so it overwrites rather than failing with FILE EXISTS." },
        { "id": "cmt_qqjsff", "address": "$C082", "text": "SEI, IOINIT, RESTOR (KERNAL reinit), sub_C0B9 (calls sub_F48A), sets border+bg to black, then SETLFS device 8 secondary 0, SETNAM len $12 name at $C100 = \"@:ATTACK MUTANT.HI\" (the @: prefix means replace-on-save), SAVE of $5E00-$5ED0 (208 bytes, ZP pointer pair at $FB/$FC) via SAVE. Only inbound reference is a JMP from loc_9F90 at $9FE6 -- reached from the game-over/high-score path. Only caller found by find_references (absolute addressing only, per its own caveat)." },
        { "id": "cmt_j80ugs", "address": "$C0B9", "text": "JSR sub_F48A; JSR CINT_IMPL (KERNAL screen init); sets IRQ vector ($0314/5) to $EA31, the stock KERNAL IRQ continuation -- i.e. this restores the default IRQ rather than installing a custom one. CLI, RTS." },
        { "id": "cmt_r5zi9z", "address": "$C0B9", "text": "Put the KERNAL back: IOINIT, RESTOR (which restores the $0314 vector table), CINT (screen editor init), then CINV = $EA31, the standard IRQ. Called only from the save path." },
        { "id": "cmt_vwk5tn", "address": "$C0CB", "text": "53 zero bytes then $40 $3A (\"@:\"). SETNAM only reads the 16 bytes at $C102 (highscore_filename), so this \"@:\" is not part of the name actually used -- reads like the start of an unused/alternate KERNAL replace-save prefix (@0:NAME convention) that never got wired up, or simply coincidental bytes. Not confirmed." },
        { "id": "cmt_p0gdho", "address": "$C100", "text": "Two overlapping filenames: $C100 is @:ATTACK MUTANT.HI (18 chars, used by SAVE), $C102 is ATTACK MUTANT.HI (16 chars, used by LOAD). The load name is the save name with the @: prefix skipped - one string serving both calls." },
        { "id": "cmt_okj0pu", "address": "$C102", "text": "KERNAL filename buffer pointed to by SETNAM (X=$02,Y=$C1) with length $10 at both $C065 (LOAD) and $C082 (SAVE)." },
        { "id": "cmt_818h4o", "address": "$C112", "text": "Ten $FF bytes then three zeros, immediately after the two filenames. Filler to the end of the loaded image at $C11F; nothing reads it." },
        { "id": "cmt_c4tekc", "address": "$C112", "text": "10 bytes of $FF then 3 zero bytes, filling out to the end of the runtime image ($C11E). Unreferenced; plausible assembler end-of-segment fill." }
      ],
